00:48:40,240 --> 00:48:42,000
where it looks like there should be an entrant

238
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:43,840
and it looks like there's money to be made,

239
00:48:43,840 --> 00:48:45,280
and yet all the firms are there saying,

240
00:48:45,280 --> 00:48:46,600
I'm worried someone else is going to enter.

241
00:48:46,600 --> 00:48:48,600
I don't know if I want to go into that market.

242
00:48:48,600 --> 00:48:49,440
And the other firm's saying,

243
00:48:49,440 --> 00:48:51,800
well, I don't know if I want to go in.

244
00:48:51,800 --> 00:48:54,240
So sometimes people thought about,

245
00:48:54,240 --> 00:48:55,080
like there's like a place,

246
00:48:55,080 --> 00:48:57,120
so who isn't that perfect for a scheme resort?

247
00:48:57,120 --> 00:48:58,840
But the problem is if there are two scheme resorts,

248
00:48:58,840 --> 00:49:01,040
then they would both lose money.

249
00:49:01,040 --> 00:49:02,280
And then each firm may be saying,

250
00:49:02,280 --> 00:49:04,720
well, is the other one going to go in there?

251
00:49:04,720 --> 00:49:06,120
I don't know, I'm worried.

252
00:49:07,520 --> 00:49:09,160
And again, in the real world,

253
00:49:09,160 --> 00:49:10,480
this is complicated by the fact

254
00:49:10,480 --> 00:49:12,680
that there might be gradually rising demand.

255
00:49:14,080 --> 00:49:16,280
So maybe that right now you could lose money,

256
00:49:16,280 --> 00:49:18,480
but if you enter now and demand keeps rising,

257
00:49:18,480 --> 00:49:21,240
then you would get to be the incumbent 10 years from now.

258
00:49:22,240 --> 00:49:24,560
Or as you wait around, then it may be too late.

259
00:49:24,560 --> 00:49:26,240
So there's that as well.

260
00:49:26,240 --> 00:49:29,280
And this is a reason why you might want to start a business

261
00:49:29,280 --> 00:49:31,760
even though you know it's going to lose money for a while.

262
00:49:31,760 --> 00:49:33,440
If you see a market that's expanding

263
00:49:33,440 --> 00:49:35,200
and you think that there is,

264
00:49:35,200 --> 00:49:36,880
that you'll eventually will be able to enjoy that,

265
00:49:36,880 --> 00:49:38,360
it's one of those situations.

266
00:49:38,360 --> 00:49:40,640
It's a reason to get in early.

267
00:49:40,640 --> 00:49:42,760
Of course, then you wind up losing a whole lot of money

268
00:49:42,760 --> 00:49:45,520
in order to capture those eventual monopoly profits.

269
00:49:48,160 --> 00:49:49,680
All right, questions?

270
00:49:49,680 --> 00:49:51,120
So I'm not going to solve this one.

271
00:49:51,120 --> 00:49:52,680
I'm going to leave you to solve it on the homework,

272
00:49:52,680 --> 00:49:55,880
but this is the setup and the intuition.

273
00:49:55,880 --> 00:49:56,720
Questions?

274
00:49:57,840 --> 00:49:58,680
Yes.

275
00:49:58,680 --> 00:50:02,800
Well, I guess in the first dot-com bubble situation,

276
00:50:02,800 --> 00:50:04,200
it just seems like there's,

277
00:50:06,200 --> 00:50:07,760
the profits aren't known,

278
00:50:07,760 --> 00:50:10,760
and it seems like there was a whole complete gamble

279
00:50:11,800 --> 00:50:13,520
of who enters what.

280
00:50:13,520 --> 00:50:15,640
Right, although there's still the question of,

281
00:50:15,640 --> 00:50:16,560
whatever the profits are,

282
00:50:16,560 --> 00:50:19,240
do you really think there's room for two Amazons?

283
00:50:19,240 --> 00:50:20,080
Right.

284
00:50:20,080 --> 00:50:22,440
Which maybe, which of course, in 2000,

285
00:50:22,440 --> 00:50:25,080
I have someone say, yeah, why wouldn't there be?

286
00:50:25,080 --> 00:50:27,640
And yet it turns out that it didn't happen that way.

287
00:50:29,680 --> 00:50:31,360
So yeah, so I mean, of course,

288
00:50:31,360 --> 00:50:33,400
these are all models where we're setting things up,

289
00:50:33,400 --> 00:50:35,240
where it's all clear what's going on.

290
00:50:35,240 --> 00:50:36,520
And if you want to say why the model

291
00:50:36,520 --> 00:50:37,360
doesn't work in the real world,

292
00:50:37,360 --> 00:50:38,200
you can just say people didn't know

293
00:50:38,200 --> 00:50:39,800
which model they were in.

294
00:50:39,800 --> 00:50:41,200
So there's always that.

295
00:50:41,200 --> 00:50:43,320
Although, once you have mixed strategy

296
00:50:43,320 --> 00:50:44,760
and Nash equilibrium, there's also,

297
00:50:44,760 --> 00:50:45,920
well, maybe they knew the model,

298
00:50:45,920 --> 00:50:47,720
but even the model itself predicts

299
00:50:47,720 --> 00:50:49,360
that there'll be confusion.

300
00:50:49,360 --> 00:50:51,640
Right, and basically that model says,

301
00:50:51,640 --> 00:50:53,120
people can understand the model perfectly,

302
00:50:53,120 --> 00:50:55,600
and yet people will still end up missing opportunities

303
00:50:55,600 --> 00:50:57,520
or sometimes losing money

304
00:50:57,520 --> 00:51:00,920
because the throw of the dice came out the wrong way.

305
00:51:00,920 --> 00:51:02,200
It's just like in a game of racquetball.

306
00:51:02,200 --> 00:51:04,520
Sometimes both players will go and bump,

307
00:51:04,520 --> 00:51:06,200
but we'll go and bump heads against each other

308
00:51:06,200 --> 00:51:07,040
and they'll hit their heads.

309
00:51:07,040 --> 00:51:08,200
And like, why did that happen?

310
00:51:08,200 --> 00:51:10,240
Well, we're each doing random strategies.

311
00:51:10,240 --> 00:51:13,800
And this was the case where my random strategy

312
00:51:13,800 --> 00:51:17,040
and your random strategy led to two bumps on the head.

313
00:51:17,920 --> 00:51:19,400
So that's possible too.

314
00:51:19,400 --> 00:51:20,240
Yes.

315
00:51:20,240 --> 00:51:21,200
There were variants in your game too, I guess,

316
00:51:21,200 --> 00:51:22,720
for payoffs as well, right?

317
00:51:22,720 --> 00:51:23,560
We'll see.

318
00:51:23,560 --> 00:51:26,160
So, I don't think it's, not in this model,

319
00:51:26,160 --> 00:51:27,160
but yes, another way to get,

320
00:51:27,160 --> 00:51:29,400
but if you make them all richer, that's true.

321
00:51:30,280 --> 00:51:31,480
Okay.

322
00:51:31,480 --> 00:51:34,160
All right, so moving on.

323
00:51:35,560 --> 00:51:39,760
All right, so allocated versus productive inefficiency.

324
00:51:39,760 --> 00:51:41,680
So most micro textbooks focus

325
00:51:41,680 --> 00:51:43,760
on the allocated inefficiency monopoly,

326
00:51:43,760 --> 00:51:46,760
this problem here of the bad triangles,

327
00:51:46,760 --> 00:51:48,240
the evil triangles,

328
00:51:48,240 --> 00:51:52,760
where you have price greater than marginal cost, right?

329
00:51:54,120 --> 00:51:54,960
Okay.

330
00:51:54,960 --> 00:51:57,520
And this is generally called allocated inefficiency.

331
00:52:02,720 --> 00:52:04,360
And if you're ever a bit confused,

332
00:52:04,360 --> 00:52:06,360
what is the intuition about what is supposed to be so bad

333
00:52:06,360 --> 00:52:07,960
about allocated inefficiency?

334
00:52:07,960 --> 00:52:08,880
I think we might've already done it,

335
00:52:08,880 --> 00:52:11,160
but it's worth doing repeatedly

336
00:52:11,160 --> 00:52:14,760
because it's so poorly, so rarely explained.

337
00:52:14,760 --> 00:52:16,960
So, you know, get two explanations in a lifetime

338
00:52:16,960 --> 00:52:18,520
and it'll still be worthwhile.

339
00:52:18,520 --> 00:52:20,880
So again, the intuition behind what's bad

340
00:52:20,880 --> 00:52:24,040
about allocated inefficiency is best expressed

341
00:52:24,040 --> 00:52:26,680
in the Steve Lansburg essay, Why Taxes Are Bad.

342
00:52:26,680 --> 00:52:29,360
And the idea of the essay is something like this.

343
00:52:29,360 --> 00:52:32,640
So suppose that you're willing to pay $20 for a shirt

344
00:52:32,640 --> 00:52:35,520
and the price without taxes is $18.

345
00:52:35,520 --> 00:52:38,280
All right, so do you buy the shirt?

346
00:52:38,280 --> 00:52:39,120
Sure.

347
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:41,120
And how much consumer surplus do you get?

348
00:52:41,120 --> 00:52:41,960
$2.

349
00:52:41,960 --> 00:52:42,960
And how much revenue does the government raise

350
00:52:42,960 --> 00:52:44,520
with no taxes?

351
00:52:44,520 --> 00:52:45,360
Nothing.

352
00:52:45,360 --> 00:52:46,320
All right, now the government goes

353
00:52:46,320 --> 00:52:48,200
and imposes a tax of a penny.

354
00:52:48,200 --> 00:52:50,280
So the market price goes up to 18.01.

355
00:52:50,280 --> 00:52:52,040
Do you still buy the shirt?

356
00:52:52,040 --> 00:52:53,840
And how much consumer surplus do you get?

357
00:52:53,840 --> 00:52:54,800
$1.99.

358
00:52:54,800 --> 00:52:56,480
How much revenue does the government get?

359
00:52:56,480 --> 00:52:57,800
Penny, right?

360
00:52:57,800 --> 00:53:00,840
All right, now the government imposes a tax of $1.99.

361
00:53:02,320 --> 00:53:04,880
So now, $1.99.

362
00:53:04,880 --> 00:53:06,480
So do you still buy?

363
00:53:06,480 --> 00:53:07,320
Sure.

364
00:53:07,320 --> 00:53:08,600
How much surplus do you get?

365
00:53:08,600 --> 00:53:09,440
Penny.

366
00:53:09,440 --> 00:53:11,000
How much revenue does the government get?

367
00:53:11,000 --> 00:53:12,200
$1.99.

368
00:53:12,200 --> 00:53:13,960
Now, the fun part of the story.

369
00:53:13,960 --> 00:53:17,200
Government raises the tax up to $2.01.

370
00:53:17,200 --> 00:53:18,560
Price of the shirt goes up 21.

371
00:53:18,560 --> 00:53:20,160
Do you still buy?

372
00:53:20,160 --> 00:53:21,040
No.

373
00:53:21,040 --> 00:53:22,280
What happens to your consumer surplus?

374
00:53:22,280 --> 00:53:23,920
What is your consumer surplus?

375
00:53:23,920 --> 00:53:24,760
Zero.

376
00:53:24,760 --> 00:53:25,600
Zero.

377
00:53:25,600 --> 00:53:26,440
What is the government's revenue?

378
00:53:26,440 --> 00:53:27,280
Zero.

379
00:53:27,280 --> 00:53:29,960
The tax has destroyed surplus.

380
00:53:30,960 --> 00:53:32,760
All right, so that is the idea.

381
00:53:32,760 --> 00:53:34,000
And of course, this isn't something

382
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:35,320
about government in particular.

383
00:53:35,320 --> 00:53:37,720
It's just something about price being greater

384
00:53:37,720 --> 00:53:39,600
than marginal social cost.

385
00:53:39,600 --> 00:53:41,800
All right, so same thing with monopolist.

386
00:53:41,800 --> 00:53:45,160
So the problem is not that you are charged,

387
00:53:45,160 --> 00:53:46,840
the problem is not that you're charging these people

388
00:53:46,840 --> 00:53:48,800
higher prices than marginal cost.

389
00:53:48,800 --> 00:53:51,800
The problem is that these people here don't buy anything.

390
00:53:51,800 --> 00:53:54,920
And that these people here lose the surplus,

391
00:53:54,920 --> 00:53:57,720
and the monopolist does not gain the surplus as profits.

392
00:53:58,600 --> 00:54:02,080
So that is what is supposed to be so bad

393
00:54:02,080 --> 00:54:03,480
about Alkerman inefficiency.

394
00:54:04,800 --> 00:54:06,160
Now, when economists started trying

395
00:54:06,160 --> 00:54:08,280
to actually measure these triangles,

396
00:54:08,280 --> 00:54:12,040
there were like a decade of being terrified of triangles.

397
00:54:12,040 --> 00:54:14,120
And then finally people started measuring them.

398
00:54:14,120 --> 00:54:16,160
There was a Chicago economist named Harberger

399
00:54:16,160 --> 00:54:18,240
who did one of the first main analyses,

400
00:54:18,240 --> 00:54:21,600
and it came out to a really, really small number.

401
00:54:21,600 --> 00:54:22,920
So he went and tried to calculate,

402
00:54:22,920 --> 00:54:24,320
what are all the deadweight costs,

403
00:54:24,320 --> 00:54:26,440
all the triangles in the economy,

404
00:54:26,440 --> 00:54:28,840
all the triangles that are keeping us awake at night.

405
00:54:28,840 --> 00:54:31,920
And he came up with something like 0.1% of GDP.

406
00:54:31,920 --> 00:54:32,760
And then it's like, well,

407
00:54:32,760 --> 00:54:35,400
so why do we worry about this problem?

408
00:54:35,400 --> 00:54:37,360
And of course, the obvious answer is,

409
00:54:37,360 --> 00:54:40,280
well, we worry about it because it's easy to teach in class,

410
00:54:40,280 --> 00:54:43,880
and it's geometry, and no one else has measured it.

411
00:54:43,880 --> 00:54:45,320
So put all that together,

412
00:54:45,320 --> 00:54:47,560
and of course we're gonna worry about a problem like that.

413
00:54:47,560 --> 00:54:49,880
So is the deadweight loss

414
00:54:49,880 --> 00:54:54,880
and the equal concept as the allocator, the inefficiency?

415
00:54:55,320 --> 00:54:56,160
I'm sorry?

416
00:54:56,160 --> 00:54:57,000
Deadweight loss.

417
00:54:57,000 --> 00:54:58,320
Yeah, yes, the deadweight loss

418
00:54:58,320 --> 00:55:01,480
and the allocator inefficiency are the same in this model.

419
00:55:01,480 --> 00:55:02,320
Yes.

420
00:55:02,320 --> 00:55:04,680
All right, and then there were, of course,

421
00:55:04,680 --> 00:55:06,000
some other people who tried to go

422
00:55:06,000 --> 00:55:07,680
and change Harper's assumptions

423
00:55:07,680 --> 00:55:09,600
in order to get a bigger number.

424
00:55:09,600 --> 00:55:11,480
So there was one guy in the early 70s

425
00:55:11,480 --> 00:55:13,320
who did a paper where he just assumed

426
00:55:13,320 --> 00:55:15,080
that every single industry in the entire economy

427
00:55:15,080 --> 00:55:17,680
is monopolized, and then what do you get?

428
00:55:17,680 --> 00:55:19,480
And then he got like 10% of GDP.

429
00:55:20,640 --> 00:55:21,560
Which is not trivial,

430
00:55:21,560 --> 00:55:24,000
but still he had to make a very extreme assumption

431
00:55:24,000 --> 00:55:27,400
in order to get a number that seemed moderately scary.

432
00:55:27,400 --> 00:55:29,080
And I think that,

433
00:55:29,080 --> 00:55:32,760
oh yeah, so the final answer of the,

434
00:55:32,760 --> 00:55:34,840
the final word of the debate, Paul Samuelson just said,

435
00:55:34,840 --> 00:55:37,360
well, everything is small if you divide by GDP.

436
00:55:37,360 --> 00:55:41,400
So, although he shouldn't really wanna say that

437
00:55:41,400 --> 00:55:43,160
because he's been spending a lot of time

438
00:55:43,160 --> 00:55:46,680
talking about how bad the costs of recessions are,

439
00:55:46,680 --> 00:55:49,400
and those actually do stay large even if you divide by GDP,

440
00:55:49,400 --> 00:55:53,400
so contradicts sort of like his own broader message.

441
00:55:53,400 --> 00:55:54,760
But any case,

442
00:55:54,760 --> 00:55:56,640
I think that's a good point.

443
00:55:56,640 --> 00:55:59,880
But any case, there's the,

444
00:55:59,880 --> 00:56:00,760
and then I think there's a little,

445
00:56:00,760 --> 00:56:03,160
like the lamer response was,

446
00:56:03,160 --> 00:56:06,080
well, 0.1% of GDP is enough to buy every person

447
00:56:06,080 --> 00:56:08,560
in the country one stink dinner per year,

448
00:56:08,560 --> 00:56:09,680
and that's really good.

449
00:56:09,680 --> 00:56:11,680
All right, all right.

450
00:56:11,680 --> 00:56:13,360
Anyway, but there it is.

451
00:56:14,280 --> 00:56:15,120
Okay.

452
00:56:16,040 --> 00:56:17,200
And again, if you're trying to pull,

453
00:56:17,200 --> 00:56:19,080
why is it that they're so small?

454
00:56:19,080 --> 00:56:21,520
Well, the simple answer is that

455
00:56:21,520 --> 00:56:25,880
you're only losing surplus on the marginal units.

456
00:56:25,880 --> 00:56:27,760
Right, so this would be like

457
00:56:27,760 --> 00:56:29,400
the people that don't see Star Wars

458
00:56:29,400 --> 00:56:31,720
because the price is too high.

459
00:56:31,720 --> 00:56:33,040
Who are these people?

460
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:34,200
Well, maybe like my mom.

461
00:56:34,200 --> 00:56:36,720
She's like, no, I'm not gonna really be paying $7

462
00:56:36,720 --> 00:56:38,040
to see Star Wars.

463
00:56:38,040 --> 00:56:41,600
All right, but losing the people who barely care

464
00:56:41,600 --> 00:56:44,480
does not actually add up to a whole lot of loss surplus.

465
00:56:44,480 --> 00:56:46,080
That is the intuition.

466
00:56:47,320 --> 00:56:48,400
All right.

467
00:56:48,400 --> 00:56:51,600
Now, far less discussed than productive inefficiency

468
00:56:51,600 --> 00:56:54,000
is another kind of inefficiency,

469
00:56:54,000 --> 00:56:56,120
which is called productive inefficiency.

470
00:56:57,080 --> 00:56:59,080
Productive inefficiency.

471
00:57:03,640 --> 00:57:04,960
All right, so.

472
00:57:06,240 --> 00:57:08,720
Situation is productively inefficient

473
00:57:08,720 --> 00:57:10,880
if the average cost producing a given quantity

474
00:57:10,880 --> 00:57:12,760
is above the minimum average cost.

475
00:57:14,320 --> 00:57:15,160
Okay.

476
00:57:15,160 --> 00:57:16,800
All right, now, these productive inefficiencies

477
00:57:16,800 --> 00:57:19,160
can easily get very large.

478
00:57:19,160 --> 00:57:20,400
Okay, why?

479
00:57:20,400 --> 00:57:22,400
Well, because of the geometry.

480
00:57:22,400 --> 00:57:25,520
All right, so let's imagine we got a situation here.

481
00:57:25,520 --> 00:57:27,320
Let's make it easy where we've got,

482
00:57:27,320 --> 00:57:29,840
here's the marginal cost of an entrant.

483
00:57:29,840 --> 00:57:32,560
Here's the marginal cost of an incumbent.

484
00:57:32,560 --> 00:57:33,840
Here's demand.

485
00:57:33,840 --> 00:57:37,520
But the incumbent is the brother-in-law of the dictator.

486
00:57:37,520 --> 00:57:40,680
And so he goes to the dictator and says,

487
00:57:40,680 --> 00:57:43,800
brother-in-law, for the sake of our family's honor,

488
00:57:43,800 --> 00:57:46,800
could you please destroy that firm?

489
00:57:46,800 --> 00:57:50,120
Or put jail, the jail, or put them in jail,

490
00:57:50,120 --> 00:57:52,400
or whatever you want, and he's just fine.

491
00:57:52,400 --> 00:57:55,320
All right, but still, this is the minimum.

492
00:57:55,320 --> 00:57:56,680
Here we have constant cost,

493
00:57:56,680 --> 00:57:59,080
so marginal cost and average cost are the same.

494
00:57:59,080 --> 00:58:00,920
But yeah, so here is the minimum average cost

495
00:58:00,920 --> 00:58:02,200
of which you could produce.

496
00:58:02,200 --> 00:58:05,120
But here is the actual average cost we're producing.

497
00:58:06,200 --> 00:58:07,760
All right, now, just to keep it simple,

498
00:58:07,760 --> 00:58:11,160
imagine that the monopolist of the incumbent then says,

499
00:58:11,160 --> 00:58:13,680
and just to be nice, we'll agree to be regulated

500
00:58:13,680 --> 00:58:16,520
so we can't charge more than our costs.

501
00:58:16,520 --> 00:58:20,160
All right, so in this scenario, your price is there.

502
00:58:22,520 --> 00:58:24,160
So here, price is actually equal to market

503
00:58:24,160 --> 00:58:25,720
the marginal cost of the incumbent.

504
00:58:25,720 --> 00:58:28,520
So we don't have any of that conventional deadweight cost

505
00:58:28,520 --> 00:58:30,920
of allocated inefficiency.

506
00:58:30,920 --> 00:58:33,900
And if you go to the monopolist and complain,

507
00:58:33,900 --> 00:58:35,720
they could easily tell you this.

508
00:58:35,720 --> 00:58:37,940
Look, I'm not making any profit here,

509
00:58:37,940 --> 00:58:40,320
so you have no reason to complain at all.

510
00:58:40,320 --> 00:58:41,560
Society has not been harmed.

511
00:58:41,560 --> 00:58:42,400
How could it have been?

512
00:58:42,400 --> 00:58:43,600
I'm not making any money.

513
00:58:43,600 --> 00:58:47,720
All right, now, is that correct, looking at the diagram,

514
00:58:47,720 --> 00:58:49,640
that society has not been harmed?

515
00:58:49,640 --> 00:58:50,480
No.

516
00:58:50,480 --> 00:58:52,840
Is it true the monopolist is making no money?

517
00:58:52,840 --> 00:58:53,680
Yes.

518
00:58:53,680 --> 00:58:54,500
Yeah, so you're making no money,

519
00:58:54,500 --> 00:58:56,000
but they're still harming society.

520
00:58:56,000 --> 00:58:57,320
How could that be?

521
00:58:57,320 --> 00:58:59,840
Because society lost the better deal

522
00:58:59,840 --> 00:59:02,480
that it could have had with the more efficient firm.

523
00:59:02,480 --> 00:59:06,560
And in fact, you say it has lost all of this here.

524
00:59:06,560 --> 00:59:09,680
And this, you may go back to geometry.

525
00:59:09,680 --> 00:59:11,920
Remember that something with this shape

526
00:59:11,920 --> 00:59:13,480
has a special name in geometry.

527
00:59:13,480 --> 00:59:14,560
It starts with a T.

528
00:59:14,560 --> 00:59:15,400
A trapezoid.

529
00:59:15,400 --> 00:59:16,680
A trapezoid, yes.

530
00:59:16,680 --> 00:59:18,880
The trapezoid of terror.

531
00:59:18,880 --> 00:59:20,360
So that is the trapezoid.

532
00:59:20,360 --> 00:59:23,880
All of this is lost because we have not allowed

533
00:59:23,880 --> 00:59:26,680
the most efficient firm, the lowest cost producer,

534
00:59:26,680 --> 00:59:27,640
to produce.

535
00:59:27,640 --> 00:59:31,920
And instead, he's been replaced with an incompetent firm

536
00:59:31,920 --> 00:59:34,200
that charges its own marginal cost.

537
00:59:34,200 --> 00:59:36,700
So it doesn't look like it's getting rich,

538
00:59:38,560 --> 00:59:40,240
but society is still suffering.

539
00:59:40,240 --> 00:59:44,400
So the sum of the losses of either the entrant

540
00:59:44,400 --> 00:59:48,680
or the consumers or some combination

541
00:59:48,680 --> 00:59:50,280
is this whole trapezoid here.

542
00:59:52,120 --> 00:59:54,640
And in general, when people have tried to estimate

543
00:59:54,640 --> 00:59:56,760
the size of productive inefficiency,

544
00:59:56,760 --> 00:59:59,360
it's way bigger than allocative inefficiency.

545
00:59:59,360 --> 01:00:13,700
And again, internal


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:03,440
that makes sense, because you're losing this on every unit.

2
01:00:03,440 --> 01:00:08,040
Every unit that gets produced by the incompetent firm has waste.

3
01:00:08,040 --> 01:00:10,800
So if the firm that is in the market

4
01:00:10,800 --> 01:00:15,480
is producing at 10% above the minimum social minimum average

5
01:00:15,480 --> 01:00:19,280
cost, then every unit has its 10% waste.

6
01:00:19,280 --> 01:00:21,280
And that is a problem.

7
01:00:21,280 --> 01:00:23,360
So again, if you're curious about, well,

8
01:00:23,360 --> 01:00:26,480
I guess these estimates are like 30 years old from the Robert

9
01:00:26,480 --> 01:00:27,680
Borg's Antitrust Paradox.

10
01:00:27,680 --> 01:00:29,800
Although I don't think there have been many efforts

11
01:00:29,800 --> 01:00:31,260
to make further estimates of this,

12
01:00:31,260 --> 01:00:33,760
people just lost interest in the subject.

13
01:00:33,760 --> 01:00:34,640
Same with me.

14
01:00:34,640 --> 01:00:36,400
Why?

15
01:00:36,400 --> 01:00:39,120
There's an element of fashion in academia.

16
01:00:39,120 --> 01:00:40,320
I don't know.

17
01:00:40,320 --> 01:00:42,080
This may come as a terrible shock.

18
01:00:42,080 --> 01:00:44,360
But sometimes people aren't interested in a topic

19
01:00:44,360 --> 01:00:46,360
because nobody else is doing it, even though it's

20
01:00:46,360 --> 01:00:47,240
really important.

21
01:00:47,240 --> 01:00:48,680
And sometimes people devote their lives

22
01:00:48,680 --> 01:00:50,640
to topics that are of no importance whatsoever,

23
01:00:50,640 --> 01:00:53,360
because other people are doing it.

24
01:00:53,360 --> 01:00:56,480
You can try to either game the system or approve it.

25
01:00:56,480 --> 01:00:58,840
That will be up to you.

26
01:00:58,840 --> 01:01:00,720
All right.

27
01:01:00,720 --> 01:01:01,220
Yes?

28
01:01:01,220 --> 01:01:02,960
Why do you draw your marginal cost curves

29
01:01:02,960 --> 01:01:04,600
horizontally instead of vertically?

30
01:01:04,600 --> 01:01:07,000
So the answer is that it's easier.

31
01:01:07,000 --> 01:01:08,280
And it's not wrong.

32
01:01:08,280 --> 01:01:09,760
It's just a special case.

33
01:01:09,760 --> 01:01:13,320
But yes, we could go and do these as,

34
01:01:13,320 --> 01:01:14,880
we could go and draw them like that.

35
01:01:14,880 --> 01:01:17,160
And then the diagram just gets harder.

36
01:01:17,160 --> 01:01:20,040
But everything I was saying still goes through.

37
01:01:20,040 --> 01:01:23,160
There is a common question about this, which is,

38
01:01:23,160 --> 01:01:26,280
why don't we go and say that that's the allocated,

39
01:01:26,280 --> 01:01:27,720
that's the productive inefficiency,

40
01:01:27,720 --> 01:01:30,120
and then call that the new allocated inefficiency

41
01:01:30,120 --> 01:01:31,480
or something like that?

42
01:01:31,480 --> 01:01:33,320
And the answer is, we could totally do that.

43
01:01:33,320 --> 01:01:34,560
But it's confusing.

44
01:01:34,560 --> 01:01:39,000
So it is less confusing to say that everything that

45
01:01:39,000 --> 01:01:42,280
is caused by not letting the lowest cost producer produce,

46
01:01:42,280 --> 01:01:44,720
we'll call all that productive inefficiency.

47
01:01:44,720 --> 01:01:48,880
And then we'll only call allocated inefficiency

48
01:01:48,880 --> 01:01:51,640
the deadweight costs that come from your price being greater

49
01:01:51,640 --> 01:01:53,880
than your own marginal cost.

50
01:01:53,880 --> 01:01:56,840
So now, going back to the scenario,

51
01:01:56,840 --> 01:01:59,960
imagine that after you go and put the entrant out

52
01:01:59,960 --> 01:02:03,000
of business, you say, I also want to make some money here.

53
01:02:03,000 --> 01:02:07,800
In that case, you're going to go and set the price that

54
01:02:07,800 --> 01:02:11,040
is above not only the minimum marginal cost,

55
01:02:11,040 --> 01:02:14,000
but you're going to set it above your own marginal cost.

56
01:02:14,000 --> 01:02:18,320
So if you are a high-cost firm and you want to make money,

57
01:02:18,320 --> 01:02:22,320
you need to charge a price higher than your own high cost.

58
01:02:22,320 --> 01:02:25,080
And in fact, you will go and set marginal revenue

59
01:02:25,080 --> 01:02:28,520
equal to the incumbent marginal cost, this high one.

60
01:02:28,520 --> 01:02:31,960
And then we will have two kinds of inefficiency.

61
01:02:31,960 --> 01:02:37,560
We'll have a trapezoid of productive inefficiency.

62
01:02:37,560 --> 01:02:40,200
And then we'll also have the triangle

63
01:02:40,200 --> 01:02:43,800
of allocated inefficiency.

64
01:02:43,800 --> 01:02:46,600
Is it overlap?

65
01:02:46,600 --> 01:02:47,240
No, actually.

66
01:02:47,240 --> 01:02:49,040
It's designed to not have overlap.

67
01:02:49,040 --> 01:02:51,840
So we only call the price being greater

68
01:02:51,840 --> 01:02:54,160
than your own marginal cost, the allocated.

69
01:02:54,160 --> 01:02:57,880
And then we call everything else the productive.

70
01:02:57,880 --> 01:02:58,920
Could you relabel it?

71
01:02:58,920 --> 01:02:59,560
Yes, you could.

72
01:02:59,560 --> 01:03:00,720
But it's confusing.

73
01:03:00,720 --> 01:03:01,680
It's more confusing.

74
01:03:01,680 --> 01:03:04,080
It is clearer if you do it this way.

75
01:03:04,080 --> 01:03:06,560
So what's determining that point?

76
01:03:06,560 --> 01:03:07,200
This one here?

77
01:03:07,200 --> 01:03:07,700
Yeah.

78
01:03:07,700 --> 01:03:10,440
Oh, well, so that's just the standard equation

79
01:03:10,440 --> 01:03:12,400
for the monopolist, where you put in your own,

80
01:03:12,400 --> 01:03:13,960
the monopolist's own marginal cost.

81
01:03:13,960 --> 01:03:15,240
So the marginal revenue curve.

82
01:03:15,240 --> 01:03:16,640
Yeah, so the marginal revenue curve

83
01:03:16,640 --> 01:03:19,920
is the same either way, because there's just one demand curve.

84
01:03:19,920 --> 01:03:22,600
But there are two different monopoly prices

85
01:03:22,600 --> 01:03:24,440
that you'll get, depending upon whether you

86
01:03:24,440 --> 01:03:27,720
have an efficient monopolist or an inefficient monopolist.

87
01:03:27,720 --> 01:03:30,800
So yes, an important point for understanding

88
01:03:30,800 --> 01:03:32,040
the basic theory of monopoly.

89
01:03:32,040 --> 01:03:35,200
So let's take the worst monopoly in the world.

90
01:03:35,200 --> 01:03:37,600
Does standard monopoly theory predict that the,

91
01:03:37,600 --> 01:03:39,880
what does standard monopoly theory predict they will do

92
01:03:39,880 --> 01:03:42,600
if their own marginal costs fall?

93
01:03:42,600 --> 01:03:44,600
So this is like the really crummy monopolist,

94
01:03:44,600 --> 01:03:47,280
where it's illegal for anyone to compete with them.

95
01:03:47,280 --> 01:03:50,200
And then, say, the cost of oil goes down.

96
01:03:50,200 --> 01:03:53,440
What does standard monopoly theory predict they will do?

97
01:03:53,440 --> 01:03:54,920
Lower price.

98
01:03:54,920 --> 01:03:55,600
Yes.

99
01:03:55,600 --> 01:03:57,600
Why would they lower price on their monopoly?

100
01:03:57,600 --> 01:03:59,800
Because they want to go and set a price equal to,

101
01:03:59,800 --> 01:04:01,400
marginal revenue equals marginal cost,

102
01:04:01,400 --> 01:04:05,000
is what maximizes a monopolist's profit.

103
01:04:05,000 --> 01:04:08,120
So if marginal cost goes down, even the worst monopolist

104
01:04:08,120 --> 01:04:11,280
in the world wants to cut his price.

105
01:04:11,280 --> 01:04:12,200
As long as it's profit.

106
01:04:12,200 --> 01:04:14,040
As long as it's profit maximizing.

107
01:04:14,040 --> 01:04:15,760
If it were a regulated monopoly that's

108
01:04:15,760 --> 01:04:17,280
not allowed to earn profits, then it

109
01:04:17,280 --> 01:04:20,960
would not decide to do it.

110
01:04:20,960 --> 01:04:21,960
All right.

111
01:04:21,960 --> 01:04:25,440
So now, I'm just thinking about examples of this.

112
01:04:25,440 --> 01:04:27,960
My favorite one of a regulated monopolist

113
01:04:27,960 --> 01:04:32,360
that doesn't allow competition is the post office.

114
01:04:32,360 --> 01:04:34,920
So as you probably know, it is illegal for anyone

115
01:04:34,920 --> 01:04:36,760
to compete with the United States Post Office

116
01:04:36,760 --> 01:04:39,600
for delivery of first class mail.

117
01:04:39,600 --> 01:04:43,120
So the post office first bans competition.

118
01:04:43,120 --> 01:04:45,040
But then, it has its prices regulated.

119
01:04:45,040 --> 01:04:47,720
So it is not allowed to earn really high profits.

120
01:04:47,720 --> 01:04:49,880
And this makes for great public relations.

121
01:04:49,880 --> 01:04:52,800
Because you can always say, all of these ignorant critics

122
01:04:52,800 --> 01:04:55,000
of the post office who act like they're making money, hand

123
01:04:55,000 --> 01:04:55,560
them a fist.

124
01:04:55,560 --> 01:04:56,200
We're not.

125
01:04:56,200 --> 01:04:59,240
We're just barely scraping by here.

126
01:04:59,240 --> 01:05:01,440
And the point of this is a, that's true,

127
01:05:01,440 --> 01:05:04,760
and yet you're still screwing us over.

128
01:05:04,760 --> 01:05:08,520
Because you are depriving us of alternatives that are better.

129
01:05:08,520 --> 01:05:12,520
All right.

130
01:05:12,520 --> 01:05:13,020
All right.

131
01:05:17,160 --> 01:05:19,320
Now, this also gives us some great insight

132
01:05:19,320 --> 01:05:20,840
into government created monopolies

133
01:05:20,840 --> 01:05:23,400
versus private monopoly versus market monopolies.

134
01:05:23,400 --> 01:05:27,000
So both of them have potential for allocating inefficiency.

135
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:29,200
So remember, we go back over here.

136
01:05:29,200 --> 01:05:31,680
So here, if there's a whole distribution

137
01:05:31,680 --> 01:05:33,800
of different costs, and the firm that

138
01:05:33,800 --> 01:05:37,840
happens to be the lowest cost will be the monopolist.

139
01:05:37,840 --> 01:05:40,200
And what price will they charge?

140
01:05:40,200 --> 01:05:42,320
You're already established?

141
01:05:42,320 --> 01:05:44,200
So what price will the incumbent firm charge

142
01:05:44,200 --> 01:05:46,080
in this simple contestability model?

143
01:05:46,080 --> 01:05:46,720
Marginal cost.

144
01:05:46,720 --> 01:05:49,920
Yeah, just below the marginal cost of the potential firm.

145
01:05:49,920 --> 01:05:52,560
Suppose there's 100 potential entrants.

146
01:05:52,560 --> 01:05:54,560
Do you take an average, or what do you do?

147
01:05:54,560 --> 01:05:55,920
Go by whatever the lowest is.

148
01:05:55,920 --> 01:05:57,920
Yes, the second best.

149
01:05:57,920 --> 01:05:58,480
Second best.

150
01:05:58,480 --> 01:06:02,520
Intuitively, what do you need to do to win a running race?

151
01:06:02,520 --> 01:06:05,680
You need to run faster than the second fastest person.

152
01:06:05,680 --> 01:06:06,840
Then you win.

153
01:06:06,840 --> 01:06:09,680
So similarly, to be the monopolist,

154
01:06:09,680 --> 01:06:15,160
you need to have lower cost than the second lowest cost firm.

155
01:06:15,160 --> 01:06:17,200
So anyway, we take a look over here.

156
01:06:17,200 --> 01:06:19,760
So we charge that price.

157
01:06:19,760 --> 01:06:22,280
So is there going to be any allocated inefficiency

158
01:06:22,280 --> 01:06:24,320
in this model?

159
01:06:24,320 --> 01:06:24,840
Well, sure.

160
01:06:24,840 --> 01:06:25,920
Here's your marginal cost.

161
01:06:25,920 --> 01:06:27,240
There's your price.

162
01:06:27,240 --> 01:06:29,160
So there's allocated inefficiency.

163
01:06:29,160 --> 01:06:31,760
Because you've got price greater than your own marginal cost,

164
01:06:31,760 --> 01:06:33,640
so you do have that allocated inefficiency.

165
01:06:33,640 --> 01:06:36,120
But is there any productive inefficiency?

166
01:06:36,120 --> 01:06:39,280
No, because in this model, the productively inefficient firms

167
01:06:39,280 --> 01:06:42,200
are out-competed and die, or they simply never

168
01:06:42,200 --> 01:06:43,680
form in the first place.

169
01:06:43,680 --> 01:06:47,080
And the firm that stays around is the lowest cost firm.

170
01:06:50,240 --> 01:06:52,560
So this says that with money monopoly,

171
01:06:52,560 --> 01:06:54,680
you may very well get allocated inefficiency.

172
01:06:54,680 --> 01:06:57,880
In fact, you should expect allocated inefficiency.

173
01:06:57,880 --> 01:07:01,760
Unless the two best firms in the world

174
01:07:01,760 --> 01:07:05,160
are tied for awesomeness.

175
01:07:05,160 --> 01:07:07,840
If the two best firms in the world have the very same cost,

176
01:07:07,840 --> 01:07:10,320
then you get allocated inefficiency.

177
01:07:10,320 --> 01:07:13,040
Otherwise, you always do.

178
01:07:13,040 --> 01:07:16,800
But at least then you don't get productive inefficiency.

179
01:07:16,800 --> 01:07:19,760
On the other hand, when you have a government-created monopoly,

180
01:07:19,760 --> 01:07:22,200
then if the prices are not regulated,

181
01:07:22,200 --> 01:07:25,280
should you still expect allocated inefficiency?

182
01:07:25,280 --> 01:07:27,840
So you can charge any price that you want,

183
01:07:27,840 --> 01:07:30,280
and the government makes competition illegal.

184
01:07:30,280 --> 01:07:33,440
Will you raise price above your own marginal cost?

185
01:07:33,440 --> 01:07:35,120
That make sense?

186
01:07:35,120 --> 01:07:38,080
But is there likely to be productive inefficiency

187
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:40,120
in this scenario?

188
01:07:40,120 --> 01:07:43,080
In fact, you should expect there to almost always

189
01:07:43,080 --> 01:07:44,840
be productive inefficiency.

190
01:07:44,840 --> 01:07:47,080
There's only one way that productive inefficiency

191
01:07:47,080 --> 01:07:48,440
will be avoided.

192
01:07:48,440 --> 01:07:51,680
That's if the government hands the monopoly to?

193
01:07:51,680 --> 01:07:52,440
Markets.

194
01:07:52,440 --> 01:07:55,640
Yes, the lowest cost firm in the world.

195
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:59,200
And why would it do that?

196
01:07:59,200 --> 01:08:02,560
That seems like wishful thinking.

197
01:08:02,560 --> 01:08:06,320
So it just so happens to be that all the dictator's brother-in-laws

198
01:08:06,320 --> 01:08:09,400
are the best at producing stuff.

199
01:08:09,400 --> 01:08:11,080
Why else would I make my brother-in-law,

200
01:08:11,080 --> 01:08:12,880
unless he was a really low-cost producer?

201
01:08:15,360 --> 01:08:20,760
So the idea there is that the very fact that you

202
01:08:20,760 --> 01:08:22,360
are going to the government and crying

203
01:08:22,360 --> 01:08:24,040
about needing production from competition

204
01:08:24,040 --> 01:08:25,840
suggests that you probably aren't that good.

205
01:08:29,000 --> 01:08:30,800
And that aside, there's just the fact, well,

206
01:08:30,800 --> 01:08:33,120
what are the odds that the government will pick the firm

207
01:08:33,120 --> 01:08:34,800
that has the very lowest costs?

208
01:08:34,800 --> 01:08:37,320
Unless there's a mechanism that ensures the government always

209
01:08:37,320 --> 01:08:41,680
picks them, then there is going to be a very high probability

210
01:08:41,680 --> 01:08:43,800
that somebody else gets picked.

211
01:08:43,800 --> 01:08:46,720
Furthermore, in the real dynamic world,

212
01:08:46,720 --> 01:08:51,040
suppose the government initially picks the most efficient firm.

213
01:08:51,040 --> 01:08:53,520
Is productive inefficiency likely to emerge later

214
01:08:53,520 --> 01:08:57,280
on in a dynamic world?

215
01:08:57,280 --> 01:08:57,800
Sure.

216
01:08:57,800 --> 01:08:58,300
Why?

217
01:08:58,300 --> 01:09:00,600
Yeah.

218
01:09:00,600 --> 01:09:02,120
New firms with lower costs.

219
01:09:02,120 --> 01:09:04,520
Yes, new firms with lower costs are born.

220
01:09:04,520 --> 01:09:06,160
And the government doesn't immediately

221
01:09:06,160 --> 01:09:07,480
grab the license of the monopolist

222
01:09:07,480 --> 01:09:08,880
and hand it to the new firm.

223
01:09:08,880 --> 01:09:10,920
Instead, they stand by their firm.

224
01:09:10,920 --> 01:09:15,440
They say, well, this firm represents France itself.

225
01:09:15,440 --> 01:09:18,880
So no, we can't just go and give the license away

226
01:09:18,880 --> 01:09:21,160
to some other firm, who supposedly

227
01:09:21,160 --> 01:09:24,040
has lower costs and everything.

228
01:09:24,040 --> 01:09:25,800
People act like just because it's illegal

229
01:09:25,800 --> 01:09:28,520
that we're scared of something, which we're totally not.

230
01:09:28,520 --> 01:09:31,600
So that is the idea.

231
01:09:31,600 --> 01:09:34,320
So the key difference between government-created monopolies

232
01:09:34,320 --> 01:09:36,720
and market monopolies, both of them

233
01:09:36,720 --> 01:09:39,080
allow for allocated inefficiency.

234
01:09:39,080 --> 01:09:41,280
But government-created monopolies

235
01:09:41,280 --> 01:09:44,000
are much more likely to have productive inefficiency, too,

236
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:46,760
which, as we said, in the real world seems to be much worse.

237
01:09:50,440 --> 01:09:53,280
So why don't we go and take our 10-minute break now.

238
01:09:53,280 --> 01:09:54,560
And let's see.

239
01:09:54,560 --> 01:09:58,640
So yes, you've got homework due today, correct?

240
01:09:58,640 --> 01:09:59,840
Just due today?

241
01:09:59,840 --> 01:10:00,340
Yes.

242
01:10:00,340 --> 01:10:00,840
Yes.

243
01:10:00,840 --> 01:10:02,640
So there's a look of non-recognition.

244
01:10:02,640 --> 01:10:06,520
And when you go and, yes, and when you get it,

245
01:10:06,520 --> 01:10:08,800
then please pick up the answer key.

246
01:10:08,800 --> 01:10:11,360
And also, let's see.

247
01:10:14,520 --> 01:10:17,400
And yes, also, never stops.

248
01:10:17,400 --> 01:10:21,320
The hunger for econ cannot be quenched.

249
01:10:21,320 --> 01:10:22,960
OK.

250
01:10:22,960 --> 01:10:23,480
All right.

251
01:10:23,480 --> 01:10:25,520
So moving on.

252
01:10:25,520 --> 01:10:27,560
All right, so predation, end-returns, and mixed

253
01:10:27,560 --> 01:10:28,920
strategies.

254
01:10:28,920 --> 01:10:32,080
Now, predation is one of those very vague words

255
01:10:32,080 --> 01:10:34,440
that gets thrown around a lot.

256
01:10:34,440 --> 01:10:37,640
So what insight can mean theory shed

257
01:10:37,640 --> 01:10:41,040
on what predation ought to mean, or the way

258
01:10:41,040 --> 01:10:42,520
that we might interpret predation?

259
01:10:42,520 --> 01:10:44,680
Actually, could you please close the door back there?

260
01:10:47,600 --> 01:10:49,120
Before I do this, I have to tell you

261
01:10:49,120 --> 01:10:50,960
one other thing about sunk costs.

262
01:10:50,960 --> 01:10:53,160
So you know the standard story about once you really

263
01:10:53,160 --> 01:10:55,920
understand economics, you walk out of movies freely,

264
01:10:55,920 --> 01:10:58,960
because you realize you've already made it at some cost.

265
01:10:58,960 --> 01:11:02,880
And so why sit there enjoying, or why sit there not enjoying

266
01:11:02,880 --> 01:11:06,760
a movie, when just because you wasted some money on it?

267
01:11:06,760 --> 01:11:08,360
By then, you're wasting doubly.

268
01:11:08,360 --> 01:11:12,800
Then the last time that I was in California, as I often do,

269
01:11:12,800 --> 01:11:15,720
I learned something new and strange about my brother.

270
01:11:15,720 --> 01:11:20,280
My brother is so cheap that he actually

271
01:11:20,280 --> 01:11:23,680
asks for his money back for any movie he doesn't like.

272
01:11:23,680 --> 01:11:29,480
Which means that for him, even though it is not a sunk cost.

273
01:11:29,480 --> 01:11:30,880
Now, think about this.

274
01:11:30,880 --> 01:11:33,160
So should this make you more or less likely to walk out

275
01:11:33,160 --> 01:11:34,840
of a movie if when you walk out, you

276
01:11:34,840 --> 01:11:38,080
feel like you also feel entitled to get your money back?

277
01:11:38,080 --> 01:11:39,600
Or, of course, is that what you want?

278
01:11:39,600 --> 01:11:40,960
Yes.

279
01:11:40,960 --> 01:11:46,600
So the fact that if a cost is sunk,

280
01:11:46,600 --> 01:11:48,640
it should make you more inclined to stay.

281
01:11:48,640 --> 01:11:51,400
So you might say, I paid $7 for this.

282
01:11:51,400 --> 01:11:53,160
I only like it at $2, but it sunk,

283
01:11:53,160 --> 01:11:54,680
so I guess I'll spend the last $2,

284
01:11:54,680 --> 01:11:56,280
and I'll get $2 surplus out of it.

285
01:11:56,280 --> 01:11:57,960
But yeah, if you get the whole $7 back,

286
01:11:57,960 --> 01:11:59,160
you'll walk out right away.

287
01:11:59,160 --> 01:12:00,360
Apparently, this works, too.

288
01:12:00,360 --> 01:12:01,680
I mean, the people are confused.

289
01:12:01,680 --> 01:12:05,200
But all right, strange, cheap, middle-aged man.

290
01:12:05,200 --> 01:12:08,560
I'm going to give you a little bit of money.

291
01:12:08,560 --> 01:12:09,800
Pardon me, please.

292
01:12:09,800 --> 01:12:12,080
How often does it work?

293
01:12:12,080 --> 01:12:13,840
Apparently, whenever he has.

294
01:12:13,840 --> 01:12:16,000
I think he even asks sometimes at the end of a movie

295
01:12:16,000 --> 01:12:20,080
and feels that he was there because his family made

296
01:12:20,080 --> 01:12:21,640
him stay there.

297
01:12:21,640 --> 01:12:23,440
Well, I won't ask to bring it for my wife.

298
01:12:23,440 --> 01:12:25,280
She liked it, but I want my money back.

299
01:12:25,280 --> 01:12:26,200
And I have to suffer.

300
01:12:26,200 --> 01:12:26,700
Yeah.

301
01:12:26,700 --> 01:12:28,680
Or, all right.

302
01:12:28,680 --> 01:12:29,640
Let's just go back.

303
01:12:29,640 --> 01:12:33,320
So the fact, all right, yeah, yes?

304
01:12:33,320 --> 01:12:36,040
I think it might be because some movie theaters have

305
01:12:36,040 --> 01:12:39,680
an agreement that the ticket price goes exclusively

306
01:12:39,680 --> 01:12:42,480
to the movie producers.

307
01:12:42,480 --> 01:12:44,720
Well, it's not normally exclusively.

308
01:12:44,720 --> 01:12:47,240
But yeah, it's true that at first it's usually at 90%,

309
01:12:47,240 --> 01:12:49,000
and then the percentage goes down.

310
01:12:49,000 --> 01:12:49,880
Yeah.

311
01:12:49,880 --> 01:12:50,400
All right.

312
01:12:50,400 --> 01:12:51,560
But they're still confused.

313
01:12:51,560 --> 01:12:55,040
It's really unusual to do this for someone to ask.

314
01:12:55,040 --> 01:12:56,240
All right.

315
01:12:56,240 --> 01:12:58,760
So now, going back to predation.

316
01:12:58,760 --> 01:12:59,240
All right.

317
01:12:59,240 --> 01:13:01,160
So the very simplest model of predation

318
01:13:01,160 --> 01:13:04,960
is just that it is a hostile phrase that

319
01:13:04,960 --> 01:13:06,800
means limit pricing.

320
01:13:06,800 --> 01:13:09,480
All right, so imagine we've got a world just like this one.

321
01:13:09,480 --> 01:13:12,040
We already drew it, where we've got lots of different cost

322
01:13:12,040 --> 01:13:14,880
curves, marginal cost one, two, marginal cost three,

323
01:13:14,880 --> 01:13:17,920
marginal cost four, marginal cost five, so on.

324
01:13:17,920 --> 01:13:19,800
It's all the different costs.

325
01:13:19,800 --> 01:13:22,600
And the most efficient firms charge the price

326
01:13:22,600 --> 01:13:26,080
just low enough to keep everyone else out of the market.

327
01:13:26,080 --> 01:13:26,920
All right.

328
01:13:26,920 --> 01:13:32,840
So now, you can understand why you might call it predation,

329
01:13:32,840 --> 01:13:34,560
because you charge the price low enough

330
01:13:34,560 --> 01:13:37,760
to make it impossible for anyone to profitably compete with you.

331
01:13:37,760 --> 01:13:40,600
It sounds kind of like you're being a predator, a wolf.

332
01:13:40,600 --> 01:13:42,360
You're like, argh, all right, I'm

333
01:13:42,360 --> 01:13:45,320
going to charge a price so none of you will be able to compete.

334
01:13:45,320 --> 01:13:49,120
I'm still profitable, but none of you are.

335
01:13:49,120 --> 01:13:49,880
All right.

336
01:13:49,880 --> 01:13:53,440
Now, in the real world, this probably happens all the time.

337
01:13:53,440 --> 01:13:57,200
As to whether or not it is done self-consciously

338
01:13:57,200 --> 01:13:58,840
is an interesting question.

339
01:13:58,840 --> 01:14:00,000
So who really cares?

340
01:14:00,000 --> 01:14:01,920
Well, the law cares a lot.

341
01:14:01,920 --> 01:14:04,400
So if you are suing someone under the antitrust law,

342
01:14:04,400 --> 01:14:07,560
and you've got to remember the way I'm going to show you,

343
01:14:07,560 --> 01:14:10,560
so suppose you can either sue someone

344
01:14:10,560 --> 01:14:14,040
for breach of contract or for breaking the antitrust laws.

345
01:14:14,040 --> 01:14:16,400
Which one do you definitely want to sue them under?

346
01:14:16,400 --> 01:14:17,760
Breach of contract.

347
01:14:17,760 --> 01:14:19,480
No, breach of contract.

348
01:14:19,480 --> 01:14:22,360
And age old law says that if you prove that they breach

349
01:14:22,360 --> 01:14:25,360
contract, they have to pay you their wages.

350
01:14:25,360 --> 01:14:28,200
But if you can sue them under antitrust law,

351
01:14:28,200 --> 01:14:30,640
then what do you get?

352
01:14:30,640 --> 01:14:32,560
Treble damages.

353
01:14:32,560 --> 01:14:33,520
Treble damages.

354
01:14:33,520 --> 01:14:37,520
So I believe that's originally in the Clayton Act.

355
01:14:37,520 --> 01:14:41,040
And this is why one of the most common kinds of private antitrust

356
01:14:41,040 --> 01:14:45,560
cases is actually franchisees suing the mother corporation.

357
01:14:45,560 --> 01:14:47,680
Because if you sue the mother corporation for breach

358
01:14:47,680 --> 01:14:49,560
of contract, you only get your money back.

359
01:14:49,560 --> 01:14:51,260
But if you can sell them under antitrust,

360
01:14:51,260 --> 01:14:53,480
you get three times the whatever harm they did to you.

361
01:14:56,080 --> 01:14:58,560
Now, so why are we talking about this?

362
01:14:58,560 --> 01:15:03,600
Because in the eyes of the law, intent matters a lot.

363
01:15:03,600 --> 01:15:05,520
Because remember, whenever you are actually

364
01:15:05,520 --> 01:15:07,720
taking someone to trial, you need to convince 12 people not

365
01:15:07,720 --> 01:15:10,520
smart enough to get out of jury duty.

366
01:15:10,520 --> 01:15:14,800
And therefore, what is likely to be very convincing to a jury

367
01:15:14,800 --> 01:15:15,320
like this?

368
01:15:15,320 --> 01:15:17,560
A bunch of diagrams and statistics and stuff?

369
01:15:17,560 --> 01:15:18,360
No.

370
01:15:18,360 --> 01:15:21,480
Far better is you want a one memo taken out

371
01:15:21,480 --> 01:15:25,040
of the archives of the accused firm that says,

372
01:15:25,040 --> 01:15:27,040
we will destroy the competition!

373
01:15:27,040 --> 01:15:28,600
Exclamation point.

374
01:15:28,600 --> 01:15:30,240
And then you wave it around and say,

375
01:15:30,240 --> 01:15:32,040
we don't need to talk about bad statistics.

376
01:15:32,040 --> 01:15:34,520
We have it in there from their own mouths.

377
01:15:34,520 --> 01:15:37,120
And that is where your lawyer's like, oh, god.

378
01:15:37,120 --> 01:15:39,320
Let's settle this now.

379
01:15:39,320 --> 01:15:42,080
Because in the eyes of the law, as well as

380
01:15:42,080 --> 01:15:44,640
the human beings that are involved in the law,

381
01:15:44,640 --> 01:15:47,000
intentions are enormously important,

382
01:15:47,000 --> 01:15:50,520
even though economically you might do this just because you

383
01:15:50,520 --> 01:15:51,020
are civil.

384
01:15:51,020 --> 01:15:52,880
Like, we just chose the brightest and nicest

385
01:15:52,880 --> 01:15:53,480
vice-profits.

386
01:15:53,480 --> 01:15:54,120
I don't know.

387
01:15:54,120 --> 01:15:55,720
I'm not trying to hurt anybody.

388
01:15:55,720 --> 01:15:57,560
And then there might be another person,

389
01:15:57,560 --> 01:16:01,280
say, like a former athlete, who's like, yeah, crush them!

390
01:16:01,280 --> 01:16:01,960
Crush them!

391
01:16:01,960 --> 01:16:04,080
Crack them in the ground!

392
01:16:04,080 --> 01:16:07,920
And the behavior is identical in both cases.

393
01:16:07,920 --> 01:16:09,760
But the attitude is different.

394
01:16:09,760 --> 01:16:14,480
And if you put it in writing, then the legal outcome

395
01:16:14,480 --> 01:16:16,120
is likely to be very different, too.

396
01:16:20,480 --> 01:16:23,800
So you're saying this is illegal under antitrust

397
01:16:23,800 --> 01:16:27,240
to intentionally undercut the prices of your company?

398
01:16:27,240 --> 01:16:31,040
Well, so if you do it and you very clearly say,

399
01:16:31,040 --> 01:16:32,640
we are doing it in order to destroy,

400
01:16:32,640 --> 01:16:35,760
in order to acquire a monopoly and destroy the competition,

401
01:16:35,760 --> 01:16:39,360
then you are likely to be in big trouble.

402
01:16:39,360 --> 01:16:41,080
Of course, as to what happens, again,

403
01:16:41,080 --> 01:16:44,120
it always depends upon what the jury will find.

404
01:16:44,120 --> 01:16:47,120
The jury is generally so economically ignorant,

405
01:16:47,120 --> 01:16:49,320
I mean, to an almost unbelievable degree.

406
01:16:49,320 --> 01:16:50,720
And yet, they are making decisions

407
01:16:50,720 --> 01:16:52,640
that could cost billions of dollars.

408
01:16:52,640 --> 01:16:57,240
So of course, in most cases, they're a lot smaller than that.

409
01:16:57,240 --> 01:16:59,960
Yeah, so even like the biggest antitrust cases,

410
01:16:59,960 --> 01:17:01,960
those are just regular old juries.

411
01:17:01,960 --> 01:17:03,360
They don't need to go and even go

412
01:17:03,360 --> 01:17:05,400
get a bachelor's degree in economics

413
01:17:05,400 --> 01:17:07,960
to sit on the Microsoft case.

414
01:17:07,960 --> 01:17:11,160
They sit there doing, well, I don't know a lot about this.

415
01:17:11,160 --> 01:17:17,040
But what I do know is Microsoft is good.

416
01:17:17,040 --> 01:17:19,560
And then they fail.

417
01:17:19,560 --> 01:17:20,320
Or bad.

418
01:17:20,320 --> 01:17:21,120
And then they lose.

419
01:17:24,800 --> 01:17:26,800
So this kind of thing probably happens all the time,

420
01:17:26,800 --> 01:17:28,280
with or without predatory intent,

421
01:17:28,280 --> 01:17:31,640
although the intent matters a lot if you're getting sued.

422
01:17:31,640 --> 01:17:34,480
Now, a more interesting model than this,

423
01:17:34,480 --> 01:17:37,200
this one is so basic that I'm going to admit

424
01:17:37,200 --> 01:17:38,680
that it probably happens a lot.

425
01:17:38,680 --> 01:17:40,840
But a more interesting model is something like this.

426
01:17:40,840 --> 01:17:44,960
The incumbent has entry-dependent prices.

427
01:17:44,960 --> 01:17:47,000
Where, so the incumbent has a policy, well,

428
01:17:47,000 --> 01:17:47,720
here's what I do.

429
01:17:47,720 --> 01:17:49,400
I price high if there's no entrant,

430
01:17:49,400 --> 01:17:50,800
and low if there's an entrant.

431
01:17:50,800 --> 01:17:53,160
And then the entrant decides whether or not to enter.

432
01:17:53,160 --> 01:17:54,680
And of course, if you are the entrant

433
01:17:54,680 --> 01:17:57,680
and the incumbent has convinced you it's going to do this,

434
01:17:57,680 --> 01:17:59,200
again, you will look and you'll see

435
01:17:59,200 --> 01:18:00,640
that he's making a lot of money right now.

436
01:18:00,640 --> 01:18:01,800
He said, well, yeah, but I'm not going

437
01:18:01,800 --> 01:18:03,440
to make a lot of money if I go in,

438
01:18:03,440 --> 01:18:05,440
because the incumbent is going to change what he does if I go

439
01:18:05,440 --> 01:18:08,600
in, and then there's no point of entering.

440
01:18:08,600 --> 01:18:10,360
So, and remember, we already talked

441
01:18:10,360 --> 01:18:11,680
about this model earlier on.

442
01:18:11,680 --> 01:18:14,720
So this is often called the entry deterrence game.

443
01:18:14,720 --> 01:18:20,320
So here's one where we got the options of in or out

444
01:18:20,320 --> 01:18:21,480
for the entrant.

445
01:18:21,480 --> 01:18:27,760
And then we have the options of accommodate or fight.

446
01:18:27,760 --> 01:18:30,920
And then over here, the entrant makes nothing,

447
01:18:30,920 --> 01:18:33,400
and the monopolist makes the monopoly profits.

448
01:18:33,400 --> 01:18:36,800
And then over here, if the entrant goes in

449
01:18:36,800 --> 01:18:39,160
and the incumbent decides to fight,

450
01:18:39,160 --> 01:18:43,280
then they both make minus A, minus A.

451
01:18:43,280 --> 01:18:45,360
And then on the other hand, if the entrant goes in

452
01:18:45,360 --> 01:18:48,320
and they accommodate, then maybe they each make,

453
01:18:48,320 --> 01:18:53,200
say, one third of the monopoly profits each,

454
01:18:53,200 --> 01:18:55,640
something like that.

455
01:18:55,640 --> 01:18:57,920
And as you may recall, there is a problem

456
01:18:57,920 --> 01:19:02,040
with this story, which is that this equilibrium here,

457
01:19:02,040 --> 01:19:05,480
this one over here, is not something perfect,

458
01:19:05,480 --> 01:19:08,080
because the incumbent would not want to fight once

459
01:19:08,080 --> 01:19:10,200
the entrant actually enters.

460
01:19:10,200 --> 01:19:13,840
So that is the problem with this.

461
01:19:13,840 --> 01:19:16,560
So this would be a case of the incumbent cutting off

462
01:19:16,560 --> 01:19:18,880
his nose to spite his face, because once the entrant has

463
01:19:18,880 --> 01:19:20,880
already entered, why not just go and settle

464
01:19:20,880 --> 01:19:22,420
for a third of the monopoly profits?

465
01:19:22,420 --> 01:19:24,760
Why insist on getting revenge and saying,

466
01:19:24,760 --> 01:19:28,280
I'm going to hurt you for doing this?

467
01:19:28,280 --> 01:19:32,280
Now, if you had a good teacher for industrialization

468
01:19:32,280 --> 01:19:34,480
at some point who talked about predation,

469
01:19:34,480 --> 01:19:36,560
they will have told you a bunch of other problems

470
01:19:36,560 --> 01:19:38,040
with standard predation stories.

471
01:19:38,040 --> 01:19:39,480
And they might have even told you

472
01:19:39,480 --> 01:19:42,000
about some actual economic history of predation,

473
01:19:42,000 --> 01:19:46,000
like what actually happened with standard oil, things like that.

474
01:19:46,000 --> 01:19:46,960
All right.

475
01:19:46,960 --> 01:19:49,480
So in this less formal literature that's out there,

476
01:19:49,480 --> 01:19:52,040
but it's worth knowing that it exists,

477
01:19:52,040 --> 01:19:54,400
people often emphasize that predation is especially

478
01:19:54,400 --> 01:19:56,280
costly for the incumbents.

479
01:19:56,280 --> 01:19:56,880
And why?

480
01:19:56,880 --> 01:19:59,480
Well, if you have 95% of the markets,

481
01:19:59,480 --> 01:20:27,460
and then you can predatorize like a dog towards $1,000,


1
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,440
go and start selling in a loss because one firm enters and gets 5% of the market, what

2
01:20:06,440 --> 01:20:08,960
do your losses look like compared to the entrance losses?

3
01:20:08,960 --> 01:20:14,440
At least 19 times as large.

4
01:20:14,440 --> 01:20:18,120
So if you are the huge firm and you say I'm going to sell everything in a loss to drive

5
01:20:18,120 --> 01:20:23,200
you out of business, if the entrant is small and you're big, you are losing way more money

6
01:20:23,200 --> 01:20:24,920
in absolute terms than he is.

7
01:20:24,920 --> 01:20:31,440
It's even worse than it seems because if you go and cut your price, then what will normally

8
01:20:31,440 --> 01:20:34,680
happen to your market share?

9
01:20:34,680 --> 01:20:35,680
Goes up.

10
01:20:35,680 --> 01:20:38,360
So then it's actually even more lopsided.

11
01:20:38,360 --> 01:20:45,080
You gain market share and every percentage point of that market share is poison.

12
01:20:45,080 --> 01:20:48,880
You are losing money.

13
01:20:48,880 --> 01:20:54,240
So anyway, this less formal literature just emphasizes that predation is especially costly

14
01:20:54,240 --> 01:20:55,800
for a monopolist.

15
01:20:55,800 --> 01:20:57,960
But here, notice there's nothing like that.

16
01:20:57,960 --> 01:21:06,800
Here it's just saying that predation is more expensive than accommodation.

17
01:21:06,800 --> 01:21:11,920
Now if you want to go and make this story sound more believable, what you normally want

18
01:21:11,920 --> 01:21:18,320
to do is say well look, obviously nobody wants to predate in the short run, but there is

19
01:21:18,320 --> 01:21:22,680
a long run benefit for predation, which is that you build up a reputation for predation

20
01:21:22,680 --> 01:21:24,880
so people don't enter.

21
01:21:24,880 --> 01:21:29,560
So what if this predation game is infinitely repeated, for example?

22
01:21:29,560 --> 01:21:33,000
So then predation is going to be potentially sustainable.

23
01:21:33,000 --> 01:21:37,800
Then it all depends on whether the short run cost of predation exceeds the present value

24
01:21:37,800 --> 01:21:42,200
of the extra monopoly profits you get as a result of predating.

25
01:21:42,200 --> 01:21:48,560
Right in here, all of the classic arguments of the good industrial organization teachers

26
01:21:48,560 --> 01:21:51,080
that you may or may not have had come into their own.

27
01:21:51,080 --> 01:21:56,560
Now these are arguments along the lines of, well look, you have to lose money, you have

28
01:21:56,560 --> 01:22:05,280
to lose 19 times as much as your competitor for a year or two, and then you have to make

29
01:22:05,280 --> 01:22:09,280
it up before another entry comes along and makes you do the same thing.

30
01:22:09,280 --> 01:22:12,760
And then there's other arguments like, suppose you're selling a durable good.

31
01:22:12,760 --> 01:22:16,640
What might your customers do during the period when you are selling below cost?

32
01:22:16,640 --> 01:22:20,040
Buy up and stockpile the good.

33
01:22:20,040 --> 01:22:24,320
Or what might, the entrant might actually say, you know what, I'm going to stop production

34
01:22:24,320 --> 01:22:26,800
and just buy your product and stockpile it.

35
01:22:26,800 --> 01:22:28,800
And I'm just going to sit on it.

36
01:22:28,800 --> 01:22:30,520
And eventually you're going to give up.

37
01:22:30,520 --> 01:22:34,480
And then I'm going to go and sell your own product that you sold to me for below cost

38
01:22:34,480 --> 01:22:35,480
to customers.

39
01:22:35,480 --> 01:22:38,480
You can do that.

40
01:22:38,480 --> 01:22:39,480
Okay.

41
01:22:39,480 --> 01:22:42,320
Now, so questions about that?

42
01:22:42,320 --> 01:22:44,720
Anyone wondering about that?

43
01:22:44,720 --> 01:22:49,040
So just to let you know, so the standard vertical and standard oil, the standard oil did not

44
01:22:49,040 --> 01:22:52,320
practice predation, contrary to what Ida Charbel said.

45
01:22:52,320 --> 01:22:53,320
Right, Ida Charbel?

46
01:22:53,320 --> 01:22:54,320
Anyone know the back story?

47
01:22:54,320 --> 01:22:59,600
What was the deal with Ida Charbel anyway?

48
01:22:59,600 --> 01:23:05,720
Her dad had lost his business and blamed standard oil, and then she goes and writes a book about

49
01:23:05,720 --> 01:23:07,280
how bad standard oil is.

50
01:23:07,280 --> 01:23:11,320
Normally this would at least be mentioned when you are conveying history.

51
01:23:11,320 --> 01:23:16,480
At least when I was taught American history, Ida Charbel's claims were presented as fact.

52
01:23:16,480 --> 01:23:19,360
Even though she did have an obvious ax to grind.

53
01:23:19,360 --> 01:23:24,160
It doesn't mean she's wrong, but it certainly seemed wrong not to tell the students about

54
01:23:24,160 --> 01:23:25,160
this conflict of interest.

55
01:23:25,160 --> 01:23:29,160
And it wasn't done, and I'm bitter about it.

56
01:23:29,160 --> 01:23:30,160
All right.

57
01:23:30,160 --> 01:23:35,840
Now, what does seem to be true is that for a while Rockefeller would buy out his competitors

58
01:23:35,840 --> 01:23:38,680
at above market prices.

59
01:23:38,680 --> 01:23:43,480
So Rockefeller did try getting a monopoly not through predation, which he considered

60
01:23:43,480 --> 01:23:46,040
to be a ridiculous strategy all along.

61
01:23:46,040 --> 01:23:50,480
Instead he just tried going to firms and paying them more than the cost of the refineries

62
01:23:50,480 --> 01:23:53,120
for the refineries.

63
01:23:53,120 --> 01:23:57,400
And tried that for a while, and then eventually he gave up on that too.

64
01:23:57,400 --> 01:24:02,880
And I think there's actually a letter from him saying, well, it was just like blackmail,

65
01:24:02,880 --> 01:24:06,160
where as soon as you paid it, then people started building refineries purely to be bought

66
01:24:06,160 --> 01:24:07,160
out.

67
01:24:07,160 --> 01:24:10,320
So I'm not going to do that.

68
01:24:10,320 --> 01:24:15,880
And so his market share had declined quite a bit, and then even long before the antitrust

69
01:24:15,880 --> 01:24:19,200
case, which dragged on for a long time.

70
01:24:19,200 --> 01:24:22,440
By the way, do you know, like what product was Standard Oil even selling?

71
01:24:22,440 --> 01:24:26,000
So yes, it was oil refinery, but this was before the automobile.

72
01:24:26,000 --> 01:24:27,560
Kerosene for lighting.

73
01:24:27,560 --> 01:24:32,520
So I think this story also is very misleading when you tell it to students today, because

74
01:24:32,520 --> 01:24:36,480
students assume that it was being used for automobile fuel, and they think about one

75
01:24:36,480 --> 01:24:41,520
firm having a monopoly or near monopoly over gasoline, and that sounds really scary.

76
01:24:41,520 --> 01:24:44,560
And then they're like, well, wait, no, it was just kerosene.

77
01:24:44,560 --> 01:24:47,160
At a time when the light bulb was spreading.

78
01:24:47,160 --> 01:24:50,840
So it doesn't actually sound all that scary at all.

79
01:24:50,840 --> 01:24:56,680
In fact, it sounds kind of like having a monopoly over like giant bulky desktop computers when

80
01:24:56,680 --> 01:24:59,600
tablets were coming along or something like that.

81
01:24:59,600 --> 01:25:02,520
All right, so questions about predation?

82
01:25:02,520 --> 01:25:03,520
All right.

83
01:25:03,520 --> 01:25:08,280
But anyway, you can go and take those payoffs, and then you can go and do the repeated game

84
01:25:08,280 --> 01:25:11,640
calculation just like I was showing you last week.

85
01:25:11,640 --> 01:25:15,800
And then you'll be able to find a critical beta where it would be worth practicing predation

86
01:25:15,800 --> 01:25:20,280
in order to, as long as the benefits actually were not.

87
01:25:20,280 --> 01:25:22,560
Oh, I have a question.

88
01:25:22,560 --> 01:25:29,120
I had thought that, for instance, in the railroad industry, the idea of the predation would

89
01:25:29,120 --> 01:25:33,240
be like if you had a huge network, rail network, right?

90
01:25:33,240 --> 01:25:39,160
And someone came along with a small point-to-point sort of rail network, you would drop the price

91
01:25:39,160 --> 01:25:44,440
on that single line, and then when they went out of business, then you use the profits

92
01:25:44,440 --> 01:25:45,440
from the rails.

93
01:25:45,440 --> 01:25:46,440
Yeah, yeah.

94
01:25:46,440 --> 01:25:49,280
So that is more, you are correct, that is a more sophisticated version of saying that

95
01:25:49,280 --> 01:25:51,520
you might just go and do that in one market.

96
01:25:51,520 --> 01:25:55,120
Although, that actually leads us right into the next question, which is why assume that

97
01:25:55,120 --> 01:25:57,680
the entrant is small to begin with?

98
01:25:57,680 --> 01:26:00,960
Why might it not be a big firm that's going to go and try to compete with you in a bunch

99
01:26:00,960 --> 01:26:02,040
of places?

100
01:26:02,040 --> 01:26:04,680
And then that strategy does not work.

101
01:26:04,680 --> 01:26:07,800
It doesn't have the same advantage.

102
01:26:07,800 --> 01:26:11,720
So general question, so why can't two play the predation game?

103
01:26:11,720 --> 01:26:15,360
So why can't an entrant predate against incumbents the same way that incumbents predate against

104
01:26:15,360 --> 01:26:16,360
entrants?

105
01:26:16,360 --> 01:26:21,640
And again, the natural solution, again, is think about it in terms of strategies.

106
01:26:21,640 --> 01:26:27,800
You say, look, there's, both firms could practice predation, in which case they both lose money.

107
01:26:27,800 --> 01:26:28,800
Or one does, one doesn't.

108
01:26:28,800 --> 01:26:34,320
And then in that case, the firm that is threatening predation gets a monopoly, and the other one

109
01:26:34,320 --> 01:26:35,520
gets nothing.

110
01:26:35,520 --> 01:26:39,800
Or maybe they're both so scared that they're going to get in over their heads that they

111
01:26:39,800 --> 01:26:40,800
don't enter at all.

112
01:26:40,800 --> 01:26:49,080
But essentially, this is a case where the potential monopoly profits from here balance

113
01:26:49,080 --> 01:26:53,840
out the losses of what's sometimes called destructive or dog-eat-dog competition, where

114
01:26:53,840 --> 01:26:58,280
you get two firms that are competing very heavily in an industry where there's some

115
01:26:58,280 --> 01:26:59,280
costs.

116
01:26:59,280 --> 01:27:05,280
And then things work out badly for both of them.

117
01:27:05,280 --> 01:27:11,680
And furthermore, if people have thought a bit more about the logic of predation, consider

118
01:27:11,680 --> 01:27:12,680
this.

119
01:27:12,680 --> 01:27:17,200
So with predation, as I say, if you are much bigger than your competitor, then you're actually

120
01:27:17,200 --> 01:27:19,920
losing a lot more money in absolute terms.

121
01:27:19,920 --> 01:27:25,480
Now suppose that rather than competing with low prices or selling below costs, you were

122
01:27:25,480 --> 01:27:28,760
to go and try suing them.

123
01:27:28,760 --> 01:27:34,560
Right now, the cost for a really big firm to sue a really small firm, does the big firm

124
01:27:34,560 --> 01:27:38,640
have to pay proportionately more for the legal costs?

125
01:27:38,640 --> 01:27:39,640
No.

126
01:27:39,640 --> 01:27:42,960
So that seems like a much more reasonable way to try to practice predation is through

127
01:27:42,960 --> 01:27:45,920
the legal system, where you just go and threaten your competitors with lawsuits.

128
01:27:45,920 --> 01:27:49,800
And they can threaten you back, but if you are big and they are small, then the lawsuit

129
01:27:49,800 --> 01:27:54,280
would be a much smaller percentage of your profits than of theirs.

130
01:27:54,280 --> 01:27:55,280
So that might be a good thing.

131
01:27:55,280 --> 01:27:57,080
Isn't that kind of what's going on in the tech industry?

132
01:27:57,080 --> 01:27:58,080
Yeah, yeah.

133
01:27:58,080 --> 01:28:03,480
This would be like a great model of being a patent troll, for example.

134
01:28:03,480 --> 01:28:09,440
So of course, you can be a small patent troll, who just, all you do is just sue people for

135
01:28:09,440 --> 01:28:10,440
it.

136
01:28:10,440 --> 01:28:13,960
But the more obvious one is to be a big firm, and any time someone tries competing with

137
01:28:13,960 --> 01:28:17,920
you, you sue them and say, you've infringed our patent.

138
01:28:17,920 --> 01:28:21,560
The single best argument I've ever seen against patents is a single diagram that Alex Tabarrok

139
01:28:21,560 --> 01:28:26,000
made of all the lawsuits of every major firm in Silicon Valley against every other major

140
01:28:26,000 --> 01:28:27,000
firm.

141
01:28:27,000 --> 01:28:29,880
And it's just like a giant swarm of arrows.

142
01:28:29,880 --> 01:28:34,200
So you just look at that and say, this is your system for increasing innovation.

143
01:28:34,200 --> 01:28:35,200
Everyone sue everyone.

144
01:28:35,200 --> 01:28:36,200
It seems hard to believe.

145
01:28:36,200 --> 01:28:45,840
I know it's just a diagram, but it's empirical in the sense that these are all actual ongoing

146
01:28:45,840 --> 01:28:46,840
lawsuits.

147
01:28:46,840 --> 01:28:52,720
And we said, my God, everyone is, almost everyone is suing almost everybody else.

148
01:28:52,720 --> 01:28:59,000
So anyway, this mixed strategy equilibrium seems to make a lot of sense to me, because

149
01:28:59,000 --> 01:29:01,760
there's no way that you can credibly commit to saying, look, I'm the one that gets to

150
01:29:01,760 --> 01:29:03,760
predate, and you're the one that gets predated upon.

151
01:29:03,760 --> 01:29:06,320
Or the other one would say, no, no, no, I'm the firm that gets to predate, and you're

152
01:29:06,320 --> 01:29:07,320
going to get predated upon.

153
01:29:07,320 --> 01:29:14,560
And this story says, look, the bigger the benefits of being the monopolist if you win,

154
01:29:14,560 --> 01:29:18,320
the greater the likelihood that firms will be, or the greater the risk of losing money

155
01:29:18,320 --> 01:29:21,240
that firms will be willing to accept.

156
01:29:21,240 --> 01:29:25,320
So if this monop- so you can go and solve for this yourselves, but intuitively, if these

157
01:29:25,320 --> 01:29:36,440
monopoly profits are really big, then in order to scare- then these losses are going to be

158
01:29:36,440 --> 01:29:37,680
there to balance things out.

159
01:29:37,680 --> 01:29:43,920
In equilibrium, it needs to be the case that you are essentially breaking even.

160
01:29:43,920 --> 01:29:46,920
As you will see.

161
01:29:46,920 --> 01:29:47,920
All right.

162
01:29:47,920 --> 01:29:48,920
Questions?

163
01:29:48,920 --> 01:29:49,920
Questions?

164
01:29:49,920 --> 01:29:50,920
All right.

165
01:29:50,920 --> 01:29:54,400
So now, last topic for this week's notes, although then we'll immediately move on to

166
01:29:54,400 --> 01:29:56,960
the next week, is Bertrand and Curnow competition.

167
01:29:56,960 --> 01:30:01,400
How many of you have heard of these two famous 19th century French anonymous?

168
01:30:01,400 --> 01:30:02,400
So yes.

169
01:30:02,400 --> 01:30:03,400
All right.

170
01:30:03,400 --> 01:30:08,840
So all the arguments that I've been making so far rely heavily on what we call Bertrand

171
01:30:08,840 --> 01:30:09,840
competition.

172
01:30:09,840 --> 01:30:14,160
And to some degree, it also depends upon constant marginal cost pricing.

173
01:30:14,160 --> 01:30:16,800
There is a sub-literature on how much that matters.

174
01:30:16,800 --> 01:30:20,520
But anyway, not that much.

175
01:30:20,520 --> 01:30:26,280
So in Bertrand models in general, the way it works is firms reserve- firms propose prices,

176
01:30:26,280 --> 01:30:30,280
all customers buy from the lowest priced firm, and if the price are equal, then customers

177
01:30:30,280 --> 01:30:31,280
randomize.

178
01:30:31,280 --> 01:30:37,080
And then in the setup, in equilibrium, the most productively efficient firm takes over

179
01:30:37,080 --> 01:30:42,360
the whole market, and they charge a price just below the- just below- excuse me, this

180
01:30:42,360 --> 01:30:46,760
is actually just below the cost, just below the marginal cost of the second most efficient

181
01:30:46,760 --> 01:30:47,760
firm.

182
01:30:47,760 --> 01:30:52,600
And then you can get price equal to marginal cost if these two firms can produce in the

183
01:30:52,600 --> 01:30:54,600
most productively efficient way.

184
01:30:54,600 --> 01:30:56,040
All right.

185
01:30:56,040 --> 01:31:00,960
Now the Bertrand competition strongly undermines the perfectly competitive model.

186
01:31:00,960 --> 01:31:04,320
Because remember, the perfectly competitive model says that you need to have how many

187
01:31:04,320 --> 01:31:06,800
firms to get price equal to marginal cost?

188
01:31:06,800 --> 01:31:07,800
Infinite.

189
01:31:07,800 --> 01:31:11,320
Infinite is the technical number.

190
01:31:11,320 --> 01:31:15,960
And now Bertrand comes along and says, actually, infinity is more than you need.

191
01:31:15,960 --> 01:31:17,800
The right number is two.

192
01:31:17,800 --> 01:31:21,120
All right, so which is of course less than infinity.

193
01:31:21,120 --> 01:31:22,120
Right?

194
01:31:22,120 --> 01:31:26,400
And if you were paying attention, the connectability model says, you know how many you really need?

195
01:31:26,400 --> 01:31:27,400
One.

196
01:31:27,400 --> 01:31:29,000
So wow, all right.

197
01:31:29,000 --> 01:31:32,400
So one firm can be just as good as perfect competition.

198
01:31:32,400 --> 01:31:35,720
All right, which is a pretty impressive result.

199
01:31:35,720 --> 01:31:40,280
Especially if you had a crummy undergraduate textbook like I always did, where they said,

200
01:31:40,280 --> 01:31:44,440
look, oh, we got like the- the more firms there are, the better the market outcome.

201
01:31:44,440 --> 01:31:45,440
The end.

202
01:31:45,440 --> 01:31:46,440
Right?

203
01:31:46,440 --> 01:31:50,520
And most of the textbooks that I had never even mentioned, that there might be some conflict

204
01:31:50,520 --> 01:31:55,200
between having- realizing economies of scale and having lots of firms.

205
01:31:55,200 --> 01:32:00,200
So again, like if you- like taken literally, the textbooks appear to be saying that if

206
01:32:00,200 --> 01:32:04,440
we would just go and trust bust every firm down to the atomic level, that this would

207
01:32:04,440 --> 01:32:05,440
be fantastic.

208
01:32:05,440 --> 01:32:06,440
All right.

209
01:32:06,440 --> 01:32:13,800
Ralph Nader in the 70s proposed some legislation which would- which would have put like a maximum

210
01:32:13,800 --> 01:32:18,560
level of concentration at- I think it was, let's see, I think like maximum firm size

211
01:32:18,560 --> 01:32:22,680
was going to be capped at 12.5- 12.5% market share for every industry.

212
01:32:22,680 --> 01:32:27,360
There was one term saying, unless this would lead to significant cost increases.

213
01:32:27,360 --> 01:32:32,640
So but the very fact that he put this in at the end as like a special case, suggests that

214
01:32:32,640 --> 01:32:35,400
it's going to be interpreted unfavorably if it passes.

215
01:32:35,400 --> 01:32:38,520
This did not pass.

216
01:32:38,520 --> 01:32:42,800
There would have remained one good legal remedy, which is just to argue that you're in the

217
01:32:42,800 --> 01:32:47,480
industry for goods and therefore nobody has 12.5% market share.

218
01:32:47,480 --> 01:32:52,240
Right, because if you know anything about any kind of actual anti-drug litigation, your

219
01:32:52,240 --> 01:32:56,240
goal is just to- the goal of the defendant is to find the market as broadly as possible

220
01:32:56,240 --> 01:32:59,080
so that your market share is trivial.

221
01:32:59,080 --> 01:33:00,080
So let's see.

222
01:33:00,080 --> 01:33:04,680
So Visa, there was an interest case against Visa and they had some really good lawyers.

223
01:33:04,680 --> 01:33:09,480
So their lawyers successfully argued that Visa was in what market?

224
01:33:09,480 --> 01:33:15,760
No, no, no, better, better, better than all credit cards.

225
01:33:15,760 --> 01:33:17,920
Better than all banking.

226
01:33:17,920 --> 01:33:20,240
All payments, all payment methods.

227
01:33:20,240 --> 01:33:24,320
All right, so that was a fine team of lawyers.

228
01:33:24,320 --> 01:33:28,480
All right, so they were like, it's just like anything.

229
01:33:28,480 --> 01:33:30,960
Like also burger is included.

230
01:33:30,960 --> 01:33:34,680
You know, someone showing up with a bag of rice saying, what can I get for this?

231
01:33:34,680 --> 01:33:37,680
This is competing with Visa at the rice store.

232
01:33:37,680 --> 01:33:38,680
All right.

233
01:33:38,680 --> 01:33:45,680
So anyway, that is actual litigation and how it works if you're good.

234
01:33:45,680 --> 01:33:46,680
Okay.

235
01:33:46,680 --> 01:33:50,800
All right, now, so this is what the Bertrand model implies.

236
01:33:50,800 --> 01:33:55,960
And in my experience, economists who are very happy with the Bertrand model at first when

237
01:33:55,960 --> 01:34:02,040
they hear the theory, generally become horrified when they realize what it implies.

238
01:34:02,040 --> 01:34:06,840
Because again, as I may have mentioned, the typical economist is not a libertarian, not

239
01:34:06,840 --> 01:34:11,280
a free market ideologue, people think that George Mason economists represent the economic

240
01:34:11,280 --> 01:34:14,280
profession are just wrong and don't know what they're talking about.

241
01:34:14,280 --> 01:34:18,080
So again, the typical economist is a moderate Democrat and the moderate Democrat is not

242
01:34:18,080 --> 01:34:21,720
very fond of hearing the two firms is fine and otherwise, you can stop your belly aching

243
01:34:21,720 --> 01:34:22,720
as long as it's two firms.

244
01:34:22,720 --> 01:34:23,720
All right.

245
01:34:23,720 --> 01:34:32,040
So perhaps, I think actually definitely because of this result, a very different model, also

