people take the preliminary exams,

38
01:61:14,860 --> 01:61:17,860
then even the basic one is generally forgotten.

39
01:61:17,860 --> 01:61:19,460
So it's depressing.

40
01:61:19,460 --> 01:61:22,300
But don't depress me.

41
01:61:22,300 --> 01:61:24,100
So you guys can all remember it.

42
01:61:24,100 --> 01:61:26,180
No reason, just because all previous groups

43
01:61:26,180 --> 01:61:29,140
have poorly remembered it, doesn't mean you have to.

44
01:61:29,140 --> 01:61:30,020
OK.

45
01:61:30,020 --> 01:61:32,780
Now, what about Cournot collusion?

46
01:61:32,780 --> 01:61:34,500
What about Cournot collusion?

47
01:61:34,500 --> 01:61:37,860
So Cournot collusion, and what would be the trigger strategy

48
01:61:37,860 --> 01:61:38,580
in Cournot?

49
01:61:38,580 --> 01:61:40,220
It would just be threatening to go back

50
01:61:40,220 --> 01:61:43,820
to regular old Cournot behavior.

51
01:61:43,820 --> 01:61:46,820
So a bunch of Cournot firms say, let's all go and agree

52
01:61:46,820 --> 01:61:51,260
that we're each going to produce one-nth of the monopoly output.

53
01:61:51,260 --> 01:61:53,780
And then if any of us breaks that deal,

54
01:61:53,780 --> 01:61:58,740
then we will all produce the Cournot output forever.

55
01:61:58,740 --> 01:62:01,340
So there are two big differences between this Cournot collusion

56
01:62:01,340 --> 01:62:02,620
and Bertrand collusion.

57
01:62:02,620 --> 01:62:06,180
So first one is the punishments can't

58
01:62:06,180 --> 01:62:08,340
drive profits below the non-cooperative stage

59
01:62:08,340 --> 01:62:11,260
gain profits, which means, and since in the simple Cournot

60
01:62:11,260 --> 01:62:15,300
model, firms do earn profits, the threat to revert to Cournot

61
01:62:15,300 --> 01:62:16,700
is not that bad.

62
01:62:16,700 --> 01:62:19,460
Because so like in the Bertrand collusion,

63
01:62:19,460 --> 01:62:22,100
either monopoly profits or nothing.

64
01:62:22,100 --> 01:62:24,660
In the Cournot collusion, either monopoly profits

65
01:62:24,660 --> 01:62:26,060
or Cournot profits.

66
01:62:26,060 --> 01:62:29,220
So the punishment isn't as bad in Cournot.

67
01:62:29,220 --> 01:62:32,300
But in Bertrand, the defector gets

68
01:62:32,300 --> 01:62:34,020
to steal the entire market.

69
01:62:34,020 --> 01:62:35,540
But in Cournot, the defector does not

70
01:62:35,540 --> 01:62:36,840
get to steal the entire market.

71
01:62:36,840 --> 01:62:40,860
Because in Cournot, a defector just produces a larger output.

72
01:62:40,860 --> 01:62:43,860
And when you produce a larger output, you get more money,

73
01:62:43,860 --> 01:62:46,460
but you don't get all the money.

74
01:62:46,460 --> 01:62:49,660
So if you sell your oil for a penny less per barrel

75
01:62:49,660 --> 01:62:52,100
than everybody else, then everybody buys your oil.

76
01:62:52,100 --> 01:62:54,380
But if you just produce double your quota,

77
01:62:54,380 --> 01:62:58,100
everybody, you are not the other oil, gets sold too.

78
01:62:58,100 --> 01:63:01,620
So we don't have to give it to them as large.

79
01:63:01,620 --> 01:63:04,500
Second, now formerly you get Cournot collusion,

80
01:63:04,500 --> 01:63:06,060
or referred to be sustainable.

81
01:63:06,060 --> 01:63:08,500
Again, on the left-hand side, it's the same equation,

82
01:63:08,500 --> 01:63:10,540
or it's the same expression, rather.

83
01:63:10,540 --> 01:63:13,860
So 1 over n times 2 from the summation from t

84
01:63:13,860 --> 01:63:18,980
equals 0 to infinity of beta over h of t times pi n.

85
01:63:18,980 --> 01:63:21,420
I guess I've been doing it as superscript c

86
01:63:21,420 --> 01:63:22,140
or superscripts.

87
01:63:22,140 --> 01:63:24,260
I'm just going to go with the new subscripts.

88
01:63:24,260 --> 01:63:25,460
All right.

89
01:63:25,460 --> 01:63:28,100
So anyway, the left-hand side is exactly the same.

90
01:63:28,100 --> 01:63:30,180
But the right-hand side is hard.

91
01:63:30,180 --> 01:63:32,020
And I think this is the most horrible algebra

92
01:63:32,020 --> 01:63:33,940
we'll ever do in this class.

93
01:63:33,940 --> 01:63:37,060
All right, so I think I've lost like nine hours of my life

94
01:63:37,060 --> 01:63:39,300
doing this, because I kept messing it up.

95
01:63:39,300 --> 01:63:41,420
Anyway, I'm simplifying the algebra for you,

96
01:63:41,420 --> 01:63:44,740
because I don't want you to suffer like I did.

97
01:63:44,740 --> 01:63:47,300
I don't think I learned anything extra from the suffering.

98
01:63:47,300 --> 01:63:50,660
So I think I had to do it with having marginal cost

99
01:63:50,660 --> 01:63:51,220
be something.

100
01:63:51,220 --> 01:63:52,980
And then that can only be by the theorem.

101
01:63:52,980 --> 01:63:55,060
I'm just going to do it with zero marginal cost.

102
01:63:55,060 --> 01:63:56,300
All right.

103
01:63:56,300 --> 01:63:59,620
So anyway, so that monopoly of profits

104
01:63:59,620 --> 01:64:04,500
must be greater than the defection profits, so defection

105
01:64:04,500 --> 01:64:09,660
profits, plus the summation from t equals 1 to infinity

106
01:64:09,660 --> 01:64:15,180
of the discounted sum of the kernel profits.

107
01:64:15,180 --> 01:64:16,300
OK.

108
01:64:16,300 --> 01:64:18,260
So that pi d is defection profits,

109
01:64:18,260 --> 01:64:21,860
and pi c is ordinary kernel profits.

110
01:64:21,860 --> 01:64:25,140
So if we use the functional forms from just a few minutes

111
01:64:25,140 --> 01:64:27,700
ago, actually, from week five notes,

112
01:64:27,700 --> 01:64:34,660
then we know pi m is just going to be a squared over 4b.

113
01:64:34,660 --> 01:64:43,300
And pi c is equal to a squared over b times n plus 1 squared.

114
01:64:43,300 --> 01:64:47,780
Just as a consistency check, remember that n equals 1,

115
01:64:47,780 --> 01:64:51,260
or monopoly is just a kernel with n equal to 1.

116
01:64:51,260 --> 01:64:54,100
So does that work?

117
01:64:54,100 --> 01:64:57,260
So if n equals 1, then kernel profits are what?

118
01:64:57,260 --> 01:64:59,980
a squared over b times that squared.

119
01:64:59,980 --> 01:65:01,180
And that's what?

120
01:65:01,180 --> 01:65:02,060
4?

121
01:65:02,060 --> 01:65:02,940
Yes, OK.

122
01:65:02,940 --> 01:65:03,940
All right, consistency.

123
01:65:03,940 --> 01:65:05,580
Good.

124
01:65:05,580 --> 01:65:06,620
OK.

125
01:65:06,620 --> 01:65:10,740
But figuring out pi d is a giant pain in the neck.

126
01:65:10,740 --> 01:65:12,700
And we're going to do it anyway.

127
01:65:12,700 --> 01:65:13,500
OK.

128
01:65:13,500 --> 01:65:16,500
So how do we figure it out?

129
01:65:16,500 --> 01:65:17,340
Why are we doing it?

130
01:65:17,340 --> 01:65:20,340
Not just to suffer, but I think it actually does teach you

131
01:65:20,340 --> 01:65:22,740
about Nash reasoning to some degree.

132
01:65:22,740 --> 01:65:23,220
All right.

133
01:65:23,220 --> 01:65:24,100
So here's the thing.

134
01:65:24,100 --> 01:65:27,740
So remember, the collusive slash monopoly output

135
01:65:27,740 --> 01:65:30,740
is a over 2b.

136
01:65:30,740 --> 01:65:31,740
So remember that?

137
01:65:31,740 --> 01:65:34,260
That is what monopolies produce.

138
01:65:34,260 --> 01:65:37,980
So if all firms other than yourself produce that,

139
01:65:37,980 --> 01:65:40,660
or if all firms other than yourself

140
01:65:40,660 --> 01:65:45,100
do produce one nth of that, then the total production

141
01:65:45,100 --> 01:65:48,060
of all firms other than yourself will be that times n minus 1

142
01:65:48,060 --> 01:65:49,380
over n.

143
01:65:49,380 --> 01:65:51,780
Like if there's 10 firms, and all of you

144
01:65:51,780 --> 01:65:54,660
say, let's each produce 1 10th of the market, 1 10th

145
01:65:54,660 --> 01:65:57,580
of the monopoly outputs, then how much do the firms

146
01:65:57,580 --> 01:65:59,420
other than yourself produce?

147
01:65:59,420 --> 01:65:59,940
9 10ths.

148
01:65:59,940 --> 01:66:02,820
9 10ths of the monopoly output.

149
01:66:02,820 --> 01:66:06,180
If there were three firms, and you all agree,

150
01:66:06,180 --> 01:66:08,300
then the firms other than yourself produce 2 3rds

151
01:66:08,300 --> 01:66:09,580
of the monopoly output.

152
01:66:09,580 --> 01:66:11,700
And then you decide what to do.

153
01:66:11,700 --> 01:66:12,220
Ha ha.

154
01:66:12,220 --> 01:66:16,220
Were they suckers, or were you going along with it?

155
01:66:16,220 --> 01:66:16,700
All right.

156
01:66:16,700 --> 01:66:21,580
So that is the summed output of everybody other than yourself.

157
01:66:21,580 --> 01:66:27,940
So what a defecting firm will maximize is going to be a.

158
01:66:27,940 --> 01:66:30,700
Remember, we have the same demand function.

159
01:66:30,700 --> 01:66:36,420
So now we've got p is equal to a minus bq.

160
01:66:36,420 --> 01:66:38,340
So we've got the same demand function.

161
01:66:38,340 --> 01:66:47,100
So a minus b times your little q plus that mess there.

162
01:66:47,100 --> 01:66:51,500
Plus n minus 1 over n times a over 2b.

163
01:66:54,260 --> 01:66:58,140
Square bracket, curvy bracket, and qi.

164
01:66:58,140 --> 01:66:58,940
OK.

165
01:66:58,940 --> 01:67:00,940
So that's what we're doing here.

166
01:67:00,940 --> 01:67:04,620
So again, this is just maximizing that thing.

167
01:67:04,620 --> 01:67:07,020
But we're plugging in for everything.

168
01:67:07,020 --> 01:67:08,460
So this is p.

169
01:67:08,460 --> 01:67:11,620
And if all firms other than you collude,

170
01:67:11,620 --> 01:67:16,420
then p is going to be given by a minus b times quantity,

171
01:67:16,420 --> 01:67:21,700
your output, plus n minus 1 over n of the monopoly output.

172
01:67:21,700 --> 01:67:24,060
And then take all that and multiply it by your output.

173
01:67:24,060 --> 01:67:26,140
And that will be your profit.

174
01:67:26,140 --> 01:67:27,140
OK.

175
01:67:27,140 --> 01:67:28,820
So there.

176
01:67:28,820 --> 01:67:30,180
You're maximizing that.

177
01:67:30,180 --> 01:67:33,260
And once you do that, then it's not that bad.

178
01:67:33,260 --> 01:67:35,420
So if you differentiate and simplify,

179
01:67:35,420 --> 01:67:39,700
then you find out that your q, your optimal q,

180
01:67:39,700 --> 01:67:45,540
is going to be a times n plus 1 over 4bn.

181
01:67:45,540 --> 01:67:49,020
And then we have to do even more algebra just

182
01:67:49,020 --> 01:67:53,020
to find out total market output is now

183
01:67:53,020 --> 01:67:57,140
the output of all firms other than yourself plus your output.

184
01:67:57,140 --> 01:67:59,980
So it's that algebra, which isn't that horrible,

185
01:67:59,980 --> 01:68:00,740
plus that.

186
01:68:00,740 --> 01:68:02,220
But summing those two things, it's

187
01:68:02,220 --> 01:68:04,740
a pretty ugly algebra, right?

188
01:68:04,740 --> 01:68:06,740
Maybe you can do this all in your head.

189
01:68:06,740 --> 01:68:07,900
I cannot.

190
01:68:07,900 --> 01:68:09,100
All right.

191
01:68:09,100 --> 01:68:12,620
And that gives us the messy expression at the bottom.

192
01:68:12,620 --> 01:68:14,700
So I'm sure all of you can just look at this

193
01:68:14,700 --> 01:68:16,120
and say, of course, big Q is going

194
01:68:16,120 --> 01:68:21,260
to be equal to a times 3n minus 1 all over 4bn.

195
01:68:21,260 --> 01:68:22,260
Duh.

196
01:68:22,260 --> 01:68:24,500
I can't do that, but you can.

197
01:68:24,500 --> 01:68:28,700
And then finally, we go back to the demand equation.

198
01:68:28,700 --> 01:68:32,180
p is equal to a minus bq.

199
01:68:32,180 --> 01:68:34,580
Plug that thing in.

200
01:68:34,580 --> 01:68:37,860
And then we find out that when you defect,

201
01:68:37,860 --> 01:68:44,620
that will drive price down to a n plus 1 over 4n.

202
01:68:44,620 --> 01:68:46,500
And here, I don't have any intuition

203
01:68:46,500 --> 01:68:48,040
to offer you other than you just have

204
01:68:48,040 --> 01:68:50,940
to work through all the algebra.

205
01:68:50,940 --> 01:68:54,300
That thing at the top, there I have some intuition.

206
01:68:54,300 --> 01:68:55,580
All right.

207
01:68:55,580 --> 01:68:57,860
So then we go and multiply together.

208
01:68:57,860 --> 01:69:01,940
We find out that we've got that p and that q.

209
01:69:01,940 --> 01:69:06,500
So the defection profits will be given by, of course,

210
01:69:06,500 --> 01:69:09,380
line i on the notes, on the second major notes,

211
01:69:09,380 --> 01:69:11,620
a squared times n plus 1 squared, all of it

212
01:69:11,620 --> 01:69:13,220
over 16bn squared.

213
01:69:16,180 --> 01:69:20,380
And now, we go back, back in time,

214
01:69:20,380 --> 01:69:23,900
to the original inequality right here.

215
01:69:23,900 --> 01:69:27,740
We go and we plug in every term, and we solve.

216
01:69:27,740 --> 01:69:30,100
So we put in monopoly profits.

217
01:69:30,100 --> 01:69:31,500
We put in defection profits.

218
01:69:31,500 --> 01:69:33,420
We put in curtail profits.

219
01:69:33,420 --> 01:69:38,740
And then we get the inequality on line j,

220
01:69:38,740 --> 01:69:39,980
for sustainable collusion.

221
01:69:42,820 --> 01:69:45,020
And I'm not going to read it aloud.

222
01:69:45,020 --> 01:69:46,780
You can take a look at that yourselves.

223
01:69:46,780 --> 01:69:48,240
Now, if you're looking at this, you

224
01:69:48,240 --> 01:69:51,300
may be getting a brief moment of mathematical terror,

225
01:69:51,300 --> 01:69:53,140
because at first glance, it looks like it's

226
01:69:53,140 --> 01:69:55,100
a fourth degree equation.

227
01:69:55,100 --> 01:69:59,300
And economists don't know how to solve fourth degree equations.

228
01:69:59,300 --> 01:70:01,780
And if you've ever looked up the formula for a fourth degree

229
01:70:01,780 --> 01:70:05,340
equation, it is multiple pages.

230
01:70:05,340 --> 01:70:06,260
It goes on and on.

231
01:70:06,260 --> 01:70:10,300
So there is a formula for it, but nobody knows this formula.

232
01:70:10,300 --> 01:70:11,940
Nobody uses it.

233
01:70:11,940 --> 01:70:13,660
And then there's a really weird proof

234
01:70:13,660 --> 01:70:15,860
saying there can't be an equation for a fifth degree

235
01:70:15,860 --> 01:70:17,620
equation.

236
01:70:17,620 --> 01:70:19,860
All right, I'll just take mathematicians' word for it.

237
01:70:19,860 --> 01:70:21,740
But anyway, you're looking at this,

238
01:70:21,740 --> 01:70:23,320
and you may be sitting there, my god,

239
01:70:23,320 --> 01:70:24,700
I must have done something wrong,

240
01:70:24,700 --> 01:70:26,740
or this model should not have ever

241
01:70:26,740 --> 01:70:28,820
been done in the first place, because it leads us

242
01:70:28,820 --> 01:70:29,860
to a fourth degree equation, and we

243
01:70:29,860 --> 01:70:31,460
don't know what to do with it.

244
01:70:31,460 --> 01:70:34,300
So instead of feeling smart, we feel stupid at the end.

245
01:70:34,300 --> 01:70:35,780
But then you go, oh, wait a second.

246
01:70:35,780 --> 01:70:37,660
We're not solving for n.

247
01:70:37,660 --> 01:70:39,140
We're solving for beta.

248
01:70:39,140 --> 01:70:41,020
And this equation is only quadratic.

249
01:70:41,020 --> 01:70:44,340
This equation is not fourth degree and beta.

250
01:70:44,340 --> 01:70:46,260
So then we're OK.

251
01:70:46,260 --> 01:70:46,920
All right, fine.

252
01:70:46,920 --> 01:70:48,380
The thing that we want to solve for

253
01:70:48,380 --> 01:70:50,340
is not actually fourth degree.

254
01:70:50,340 --> 01:70:52,660
So then we get this equation for beta.

255
01:70:52,660 --> 01:70:54,660
So and again, it's very messy.

256
01:70:54,660 --> 01:70:57,540
But if you look at it, it's perfectly solvable.

257
01:70:57,540 --> 01:71:00,900
There is nothing that you, conceptually,

258
01:71:00,900 --> 01:71:03,060
there's nothing to solving it that you haven't done

259
01:71:03,060 --> 01:71:04,380
a bunch of other times before.

260
01:71:04,380 --> 01:71:07,460
And probably actually even in first year algebra.

261
01:71:07,460 --> 01:71:09,140
And you look at it, and I will say

262
01:71:09,140 --> 01:71:11,580
there's no good intuition for this.

263
01:71:11,580 --> 01:71:13,700
But you can just go and put in some numbers

264
01:71:13,700 --> 01:71:15,220
and convince yourself.

265
01:71:15,220 --> 01:71:19,300
So n equals 2, and beta star is 0.53.

266
01:71:19,300 --> 01:71:20,860
And if you go and put in a bigger n,

267
01:71:20,860 --> 01:71:24,500
then you'll get a bigger beta star.

268
01:71:24,500 --> 01:71:27,300
So let's see.

269
01:71:27,300 --> 01:71:29,780
And I believe if you put an infinite n,

270
01:71:29,780 --> 01:71:34,340
then beta will asymptote it to 1.

271
01:71:34,340 --> 01:71:36,740
OK, so questions?

272
01:71:36,740 --> 01:71:38,020
Questions?

273
01:71:38,020 --> 01:71:38,780
All right, 7A.

274
01:71:38,780 --> 01:71:39,420
If I guess n.

275
01:71:39,420 --> 01:71:41,460
I think I can honestly promise we'll never

276
01:71:41,460 --> 01:71:45,700
have to do any worse algebra than this in this whole class.

277
01:71:45,700 --> 01:71:50,500
All right, now remember, these examples, these models,

278
01:71:50,500 --> 01:71:54,060
they abstract from a great many problems with collusion.

279
01:71:54,060 --> 01:71:56,620
And again, if you had a good industrial organization

280
01:71:56,620 --> 01:71:58,740
teacher ever, then they will tell you

281
01:71:58,740 --> 01:72:01,700
that the fundamental problem with collusion is do entry.

282
01:72:01,700 --> 01:72:03,340
Collusion attracts entry.

283
01:72:03,340 --> 01:72:04,780
And then in the real world, there

284
01:72:04,780 --> 01:72:10,100
is a great dilemma faced by the cartel, to cut the entrance in

285
01:72:10,100 --> 01:72:11,980
or not to cut them in.

286
01:72:11,980 --> 01:72:16,140
And both are evil from the point of view of the cartel.

287
01:72:16,140 --> 01:72:18,820
Because if you don't cut them in, what happens?

288
01:72:18,820 --> 01:72:20,180
They'll out-compete you.

289
01:72:20,180 --> 01:72:22,860
Yeah, they compete against you, and they ruin the cartel.

290
01:72:22,860 --> 01:72:25,340
And if you do let them in, they've got to cut the money.

291
01:72:25,340 --> 01:72:26,620
Yes, you have less money.

292
01:72:26,620 --> 01:72:30,260
And also, you have just created a very perverse incentive

293
01:72:30,260 --> 01:72:33,060
to create new firms and say, hey, let me in,

294
01:72:33,060 --> 01:72:35,980
or else I'm going to have to compete with you.

295
01:72:35,980 --> 01:72:36,620
Yes?

296
01:72:36,620 --> 01:72:39,540
Has anyone ever applied this model

297
01:72:39,540 --> 01:72:42,700
to big tent versus small tent political movements?

298
01:72:42,700 --> 01:72:45,860
Big tent versus small tent political movements, probably.

299
01:72:45,860 --> 01:72:50,580
I don't remember anything specific on it.

300
01:72:50,580 --> 01:72:53,180
Again, of course, with political movements,

301
01:72:53,180 --> 01:72:57,420
you're not generally trying to say, the thing you're maximizing

302
01:72:57,420 --> 01:72:59,500
is probably very different in the functional form

303
01:72:59,500 --> 01:73:00,660
than profits.

304
01:73:00,660 --> 01:73:03,100
So for power, for example, you just

305
01:73:03,100 --> 01:73:04,260
want to be the most powerful.

306
01:73:04,260 --> 01:73:09,140
You want to have highest relative power.

307
01:73:09,140 --> 01:73:11,780
Whereas when in business, you're concerned

308
01:73:11,780 --> 01:73:15,220
about your absolute profits, at least normally.

309
01:73:15,220 --> 01:73:17,420
There are some weird models where firms are concerned

310
01:73:17,420 --> 01:73:21,420
with their market share rather than profits.

311
01:73:21,420 --> 01:73:23,740
Although, there have to be some limits to these models,

312
01:73:23,740 --> 01:73:28,220
because what is the simplest way to get a great market share?

313
01:73:28,220 --> 01:73:30,700
The really easy way to get 100% market share.

314
01:73:30,700 --> 01:73:32,420
Just throw the new product in for free?

315
01:73:32,420 --> 01:73:34,100
Yes, give your product away for free,

316
01:73:34,100 --> 01:73:36,900
and tell you you're going to go bankrupt, at least.

317
01:73:36,900 --> 01:73:41,060
So there's something fishy about market share models,

318
01:73:41,060 --> 01:73:43,740
because it's too easy to get a really high market share.

319
01:73:43,740 --> 01:73:45,860
You might say, well, it's maximized market share

320
01:73:45,860 --> 01:73:48,180
conditional on not going bankrupt.

321
01:73:48,180 --> 01:73:51,300
I don't think I've ever seen that one tried, but you're

322
01:73:51,300 --> 01:73:53,100
right.

323
01:73:53,100 --> 01:73:54,860
So anyway, these models are totally

324
01:73:54,860 --> 01:73:56,780
abstracting from new entry, which

325
01:73:56,780 --> 01:73:59,220
in any real world analysis of collusion

326
01:73:59,220 --> 01:74:00,260
is incredibly important.

327
01:74:03,340 --> 01:74:03,840
Questions?

328
01:74:09,620 --> 01:74:11,500
And furthermore, people often think,

329
01:74:11,500 --> 01:74:13,620
well, you don't really need to worry about new entry

330
01:74:13,620 --> 01:74:15,140
when entry is hard.

331
01:74:15,140 --> 01:74:17,980
But normally, when entry is hard, exit is hard too.

332
01:74:17,980 --> 01:74:20,220
So for example, you're OPEC.

333
01:74:20,220 --> 01:74:22,220
You push up the world price of oil.

334
01:74:22,220 --> 01:74:24,900
And at first, this doesn't cause the sudden discovery

335
01:74:24,900 --> 01:74:26,020
of any new oil.

336
01:74:26,020 --> 01:74:28,340
But eventually, when the price is high for a long time,

337
01:74:28,340 --> 01:74:29,620
it does get countries looking around.

338
01:74:29,620 --> 01:74:30,620
Do we have any oil here?

339
01:74:30,620 --> 01:74:31,740
It's really expensive.

340
01:74:31,740 --> 01:74:34,140
And then once they find oil, then OPEC

341
01:74:34,140 --> 01:74:36,060
is in a worse position than it was before it

342
01:74:36,060 --> 01:74:37,900
got the price high, because now you

343
01:74:37,900 --> 01:74:42,580
have a bunch of places with new oil producers who can either

344
01:74:42,580 --> 01:74:45,100
be invited to join OPEC, in which case

345
01:74:45,100 --> 01:74:47,260
you're cutting down the share of all the members,

346
01:74:47,260 --> 01:74:52,220
or they stay out, and then they ruin OPEC.

347
01:74:52,220 --> 01:74:56,860
So according to all of the normal energy economists

348
01:74:56,860 --> 01:74:59,660
that I've talked to, so not George Mason, weird people,

349
01:74:59,660 --> 01:75:03,140
anything like that, just normal, boring, vanilla energy

350
01:75:03,140 --> 01:75:05,820
economists, I was very surprised to find out

351
01:75:05,820 --> 01:75:10,340
very standard view is OPEC accomplishes nothing on balance

352
01:75:10,340 --> 01:75:12,340
other than provide jobs for some bureaucrats who

353
01:75:12,340 --> 01:75:14,180
work at OPEC, I guess.

354
01:75:14,180 --> 01:75:17,100
So anyway, that is a very common view.

355
01:75:17,100 --> 01:75:19,380
And again, it would fit with, in the long run,

356
01:75:19,380 --> 01:75:21,820
when you go and push prices up, then all you're really doing

357
01:75:21,820 --> 01:75:24,300
is creating a period when prices are going to be even lower,

358
01:75:24,300 --> 01:75:26,420
because you've incurred this new entry, which

359
01:75:26,420 --> 01:75:27,940
took a while to enter, but now it's

360
01:75:27,940 --> 01:75:30,980
going to take a long time to disappear.

361
01:75:30,980 --> 01:75:33,140
Could that be why it persists, and why it still

362
01:75:33,140 --> 01:75:36,980
sticks around, even though it's sort of like a facade?

363
01:75:36,980 --> 01:75:41,180
They're just like, we don't want to lose face, I guess?

364
01:75:41,180 --> 01:75:43,860
Yeah, at least the usual story is it's all political,

365
01:75:43,860 --> 01:75:45,780
and it would be humiliating for the governments

366
01:75:45,780 --> 01:75:48,220
to admit that what they're doing hasn't been working.

367
01:75:48,220 --> 01:75:52,500
And again, it is an old-fashioned public choice

368
01:75:52,500 --> 01:75:56,220
special interest story, where a lot of members

369
01:75:56,220 --> 01:75:58,780
of the royal Saudi family are probably working for OPEC.

370
01:75:58,780 --> 01:76:01,100
And then for the Saudis to say, well, it's kind of dumb,

371
01:76:01,100 --> 01:76:02,620
it doesn't work.

372
01:76:02,620 --> 01:76:04,200
They are undermining their own regime.

373
01:76:04,200 --> 01:76:05,900
And yeah, they could go and buy them out,

374
01:76:05,900 --> 01:76:09,340
but then they are publicly admitting

375
01:76:09,340 --> 01:76:11,100
that they're parasites, whereas now they

376
01:76:11,100 --> 01:76:13,500
can claim that they're working for the good of the people

377
01:76:13,500 --> 01:76:15,380
of Saudi Arabia.

378
01:76:15,380 --> 01:76:17,060
Yeah.

379
01:76:17,060 --> 01:76:18,100
OK.

380
01:76:18,100 --> 01:76:20,180
All right, so questions about collusion?

381
01:76:20,180 --> 01:76:21,660
No one happy?

382
01:76:21,660 --> 01:76:24,860
OK, then let's keep forging ahead.

383
01:76:24,860 --> 01:76:26,900
All right, so I assume everyone here

384
01:76:26,900 --> 01:76:29,580
is familiar with all the basic concepts of public goods

385
01:76:29,580 --> 01:76:30,620
and externalities, right?

386
01:76:30,620 --> 01:76:32,660
You've heard this stuff before.

387
01:76:32,660 --> 01:76:36,100
All right, now many of you, oh, yes?

388
01:76:36,100 --> 01:76:39,420
I haven't come to the notes, so maybe I'm at a miss list.

389
01:76:39,420 --> 01:76:41,460
Are many of these Stackelberg-type?

390
01:76:41,460 --> 01:76:43,780
Let's see, I think that there may be a homework

391
01:76:43,780 --> 01:76:45,980
problem involving Stackelberg, but we're not actually

392
01:76:45,980 --> 01:76:47,340
going to do it in class.

393
01:76:47,340 --> 01:76:50,540
Yes, so yeah, I'm pretty sure we don't do it in class.

394
01:76:50,540 --> 01:76:54,860
But yes, with Stackelberg, it's essentially just like Cournot,

395
01:76:54,860 --> 01:76:57,300
except there's a first mover.

396
01:76:57,300 --> 01:76:59,540
So you go and you get the equation

397
01:76:59,540 --> 01:77:01,300
for what a Cournot firm does.

398
01:77:01,300 --> 01:77:04,900
And then the first mover goes and sets its output

399
01:77:04,900 --> 01:77:08,340
in order to take advantage of the other firm's behavior,

400
01:77:08,340 --> 01:77:10,660
in order to take advantage of the other firm's reaction

401
01:77:10,660 --> 01:77:11,300
function.

402
01:77:11,300 --> 01:77:14,580
And punchline, so even though people

403
01:77:14,580 --> 01:77:16,080
have looked at the Stackelberg model

404
01:77:16,080 --> 01:77:18,040
as saying, ah, this shows how things are really

405
01:77:18,040 --> 01:77:20,340
bad in this first mover, Stackelberg actually

406
01:77:20,340 --> 01:77:22,660
gives you a higher level of output than regular Cournot.

407
01:77:22,660 --> 01:77:25,340
So it's the opposite of what people want the result to be.

408
01:77:28,660 --> 01:77:31,820
All right, so we assume you've all

409
01:77:31,820 --> 01:77:34,980
heard about public goods and externality at some point.

410
01:77:34,980 --> 01:77:37,940
Now, it is common for textbooks to talk

411
01:77:37,940 --> 01:77:40,660
about two different features of public goods,

412
01:77:40,660 --> 01:77:44,340
namely non-excludability and non-rivalry.

413
01:77:44,340 --> 01:77:47,700
Non-rivalry, in my view, is just a distraction

414
01:77:47,700 --> 01:77:50,500
because we see non-rivalry everywhere.

415
01:77:50,500 --> 01:77:53,420
So you go to a movie theater in a matinee,

416
01:77:53,420 --> 01:77:55,900
look, witness the non-rivalry.

417
01:77:55,900 --> 01:77:57,940
There's a ton of empty seats.

418
01:77:57,940 --> 01:78:03,580
So non-rivalry is all over the regular for-profit sector,

419
01:78:03,580 --> 01:78:05,200
things that we never think of as having

420
01:78:05,200 --> 01:78:07,060
anything to do with public goods.

421
01:78:07,060 --> 01:78:09,140
Non-rivalry is all over the place.

422
01:78:09,140 --> 01:78:12,380
So talking about it as being a special feature of public goods

423
01:78:12,380 --> 01:78:13,820
is just distracting and confusing.

424
01:78:13,820 --> 01:78:15,940
And if we really wanted our students to understand,

425
01:78:15,940 --> 01:78:17,700
we would have stopped doing it that way.

426
01:78:17,700 --> 01:78:19,940
But anyway, all I can do is try to help you

427
01:78:19,940 --> 01:78:21,660
once you're in graduate school and to say,

428
01:78:21,660 --> 01:78:23,580
you remember all the stuff about non-rivalry?

429
01:78:23,580 --> 01:78:27,380
It really was just a distraction.

430
01:78:27,380 --> 01:78:29,940
Non-excludability is the real key

431
01:78:29,940 --> 01:78:31,460
because with non-excludability, there

432
01:78:31,460 --> 01:78:35,220
is a basic logic of selfishness that it says, look,

433
01:78:35,220 --> 01:78:37,940
if a good is not excludable, then by definition,

434
01:78:37,940 --> 01:78:41,380
there is no feasible way to exclude non-payers.

435
01:78:41,380 --> 01:78:42,860
So it's not excludable.

436
01:78:42,860 --> 01:78:45,100
So there's no way you can stop someone who has not

437
01:78:45,100 --> 01:78:47,620
paid from enjoying the good.

438
01:78:47,620 --> 01:78:49,460
Next, so that's just a definition.

439
01:78:49,460 --> 01:78:51,300
Assumption one is just a definition.

440
01:78:51,300 --> 01:78:53,820
Assumption two, though, is a behavioral assumption

441
01:78:53,820 --> 01:78:57,580
and says that since you don't have to pay to use it,

442
01:78:57,580 --> 01:78:59,500
selfish people will not pay to use it.

443
01:78:59,500 --> 01:79:01,940
And of course, in the end, there is a blah.

444
01:79:01,940 --> 01:79:04,540
I guess that's just a definition too until you add on

445
01:79:04,540 --> 01:79:07,180
the key thing, which is human beings are selfish.

446
01:79:07,180 --> 01:79:09,460
That's what makes it empirical.

447
01:79:09,460 --> 01:79:12,060
So but anyway, if you don't have to pay to use something

448
01:79:12,060 --> 01:79:14,700
and you're selfish, someone comes to you and says,

449
01:79:14,700 --> 01:79:17,140
well, you don't have to pay to use it, and you're selfish.

450
01:79:17,140 --> 01:79:18,660
Do you want to pay anyway?

451
01:79:18,660 --> 01:79:19,700
No.

452
01:79:19,700 --> 01:79:20,660
I don't want to pay for something

453
01:79:20,660 --> 01:79:21,900
that I don't have to pay for.

454
01:79:24,420 --> 01:79:25,740
And then the last step.

455
01:79:25,740 --> 01:79:29,740
So if no one will pay for it, why would a selfish producer

456
01:79:29,740 --> 01:79:31,140
produce it?

457
01:79:31,140 --> 01:79:34,500
So just imagine going and passing a hat to collect donations

458
01:79:34,500 --> 01:79:37,700
and how much money do selfish people put in in total?

459
01:79:37,700 --> 01:79:38,460
None.

460
01:79:38,460 --> 01:79:40,420
And then if you go to a producer and say,

461
01:79:40,420 --> 01:79:42,140
if you agree to produce this public good,

462
01:79:42,140 --> 01:79:45,060
I will give you the entire contents of this hat.

463
01:79:45,060 --> 01:79:47,100
And you're like, show me the hat.

464
01:79:47,100 --> 01:79:49,460
All right, there's nothing in the hat.

465
01:79:49,460 --> 01:79:51,100
All right.

466
01:79:51,100 --> 01:79:54,700
Now, there are two different ways

467
01:79:54,700 --> 01:80:04,660
you can go and diagram these non-extremal benefits and costs.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:04,280
I'm just going to do one that I like better.

2
01:80:04,280 --> 01:80:05,880
It's not the only way.

3
01:80:05,880 --> 01:80:06,880
There's another way.

4
01:80:06,880 --> 01:80:07,880
All right.

5
01:80:07,880 --> 01:80:13,420
So, the first one, so if this is standard market, QP,

6
01:80:13,420 --> 01:80:15,520
we've got supply, we've got demand.

7
01:80:15,520 --> 01:80:17,420
And then if there's a positive externality,

8
01:80:17,420 --> 01:80:20,820
one way we can diagram this is to add a social benefits

9
01:80:20,820 --> 01:80:24,460
curve on top of the regular supply and demand graph.

10
01:80:24,460 --> 01:80:27,860
And the social benefits curve includes all the products,

11
01:80:27,860 --> 01:80:30,100
all the products, all the benefits, includes the bank

12
01:80:30,100 --> 01:80:31,100
curve.

13
01:80:31,100 --> 01:80:34,700
So, that new, so customers are part of society, right?

14
01:80:34,700 --> 01:80:39,240
But it adds on this additional value from the bystanders.

15
01:80:39,240 --> 01:80:40,780
Additional value from the bystanders.

16
01:80:40,780 --> 01:80:43,580
So, social benefits with positive externalities,

17
01:80:43,580 --> 01:80:49,460
of course, exceeds the private benefits.

18
01:80:49,460 --> 01:80:52,620
And then similarly over here, if you have negative externalities,

19
01:80:52,620 --> 01:80:56,260
then we've got supply, demand.

20
01:80:56,260 --> 01:80:58,400
And now, with negative externalities,

21
01:80:58,400 --> 01:81:00,860
we draw social benefits below the demand curve.

22
01:81:00,860 --> 01:81:02,860
Because for every unit that's getting produced,

23
01:81:02,860 --> 01:81:06,000
there's some bystanders who are choking or gagging

24
01:81:06,000 --> 01:81:10,380
or looking in horror or disgust at what you're doing.

25
01:81:10,380 --> 01:81:12,100
Or disappointment.

26
01:81:12,100 --> 01:81:13,240
Percurbation.

27
01:81:13,240 --> 01:81:16,620
You may hear some very mild disapproval.

28
01:81:16,620 --> 01:81:19,180
But anyway, something negative, something negative,

29
01:81:19,180 --> 01:81:21,180
where they have some willingness to pay.

30
01:81:21,180 --> 01:81:24,460
Well, then why don't they pay if they're willing to pay?

31
01:81:24,460 --> 01:81:26,340
Because of the non-excludability.

32
01:81:26,340 --> 01:81:27,740
If one of these people here says,

33
01:81:27,740 --> 01:81:31,660
hey, I'll go and pay you guys to produce less.

34
01:81:31,660 --> 01:81:33,940
Then who gets the benefit?

35
01:81:33,940 --> 01:81:36,380
All the people who are suffering get the benefit, not just

36
01:81:36,380 --> 01:81:37,260
the payer.

37
01:81:37,260 --> 01:81:40,260
So, the marginal gain for the payer is essentially nothing.

38
01:81:44,820 --> 01:81:46,260
Now, one thing that you might have

39
01:81:46,260 --> 01:81:47,860
noticed when you listen to economists

40
01:81:47,860 --> 01:81:50,900
talk about the real world, is that they

41
01:81:50,900 --> 01:81:52,980
seem to deviate from the textbook.

42
01:81:52,980 --> 01:81:54,980
Because in the textbook, positive externalities

43
01:81:54,980 --> 01:81:57,220
and negative externalities aren't the same thing

44
01:81:57,220 --> 01:81:59,540
as public goods and public bads.

45
01:81:59,540 --> 01:82:02,060
And in fact, there isn't any clear connection.

46
01:82:02,060 --> 01:82:04,700
There isn't a clear, simple way to translate from one

47
01:82:04,700 --> 01:82:05,820
to another.

48
01:82:05,820 --> 01:82:08,620
But when economists actually talk about the real world,

49
01:82:08,620 --> 01:82:10,460
they often will talk about, they often

50
01:82:10,460 --> 01:82:12,460
will interchangeably say positive externalities

51
01:82:12,460 --> 01:82:16,260
and public goods very, very easily.

52
01:82:16,260 --> 01:82:18,940
And negative externalities and public bads very easily.

53
01:82:18,940 --> 01:82:20,620
So people sometimes say, well, education

54
01:82:20,620 --> 01:82:23,220
is positive externalities, so it's public good.

55
01:82:23,220 --> 01:82:26,380
And say, well, the textbook said those were different things,

56
01:82:26,380 --> 01:82:29,980
and kind of related, but not related in any simple way.

57
01:82:29,980 --> 01:82:33,820
So, there's two things that we could do.

58
01:82:33,820 --> 01:82:35,940
One thing is we could just reform our language,

59
01:82:35,940 --> 01:82:38,620
so that we always use public good and public bads purely

60
01:82:38,620 --> 01:82:40,220
in the textbook definition.

61
01:82:40,220 --> 01:82:42,300
And then otherwise, we say positive externalities

62
01:82:42,300 --> 01:82:43,900
and negative externalities.

63
01:82:43,900 --> 01:82:45,540
So that's one option.

64
01:82:45,540 --> 01:82:49,180
But another option is to pay more careful attention

65
01:82:49,180 --> 01:82:51,740
to how would economists do in practice.

66
01:82:51,740 --> 01:82:54,700
So when do economists say positive externalities

67
01:82:54,700 --> 01:82:57,220
versus public goods in practice?

68
01:82:57,220 --> 01:83:00,260
So I have a pet theory of this, of how economists

69
01:83:00,260 --> 01:83:02,580
use the word in practice.

70
01:83:02,580 --> 01:83:04,340
We use both words in practice.

71
01:83:04,340 --> 01:83:05,780
And now I will share with you.

72
01:83:05,780 --> 01:83:08,980
You may decide for yourselves whether this accurately

73
01:83:08,980 --> 01:83:12,260
describes usage of the terms.

74
01:83:12,260 --> 01:83:16,420
This is what I think is going on.

75
01:83:16,420 --> 01:83:19,540
So when do economists actually say public goods rather

76
01:83:19,540 --> 01:83:21,500
than positive externalities?

77
01:83:21,500 --> 01:83:24,660
So I say that economists say public goods when

78
01:83:24,660 --> 01:83:27,220
it's an extreme positive externality.

79
01:83:27,220 --> 01:83:30,060
So I say that when economists switch over

80
01:83:30,060 --> 01:83:32,580
from saying positive externalities to public goods

81
01:83:32,580 --> 01:83:35,620
when they think the social benefits are substantial,

82
01:83:35,620 --> 01:83:39,900
but private demand is basically zero or negligible.

83
01:83:39,900 --> 01:83:43,900
So this is not from any textbook I know of.

84
01:83:43,900 --> 01:83:46,460
But if you just look at the way that economists use the word,

85
01:83:46,460 --> 01:83:48,460
it seems to me that they talk about public goods

86
01:83:48,460 --> 01:83:50,980
in cases of extreme positive externalities,

87
01:83:50,980 --> 01:83:54,980
where the market equilibrium would be roughly zero.

88
01:83:54,980 --> 01:83:58,380
And yet there is some big social benefit to existing.

89
01:83:58,380 --> 01:84:01,460
And then similarly, I have an analogous claim

90
01:84:01,460 --> 01:84:04,020
for public bads.

91
01:84:04,020 --> 01:84:05,820
In practice, economists treat public bads

92
01:84:05,820 --> 01:84:08,900
as being very extreme negative externalities,

93
01:84:08,900 --> 01:84:11,940
